WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:06.135
&gt;&gt; All right. Thank
you very much.

00:00:06.136 --> 00:00:09.600
This is the second
afternoon panel.

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:10.830
We'll be hearing about fairness,

00:00:10.831 --> 00:00:12.420
and this is going
to be moderated by

00:00:12.421 --> 00:00:14.520
our very own Hanna Wallach

00:00:14.521 --> 00:00:18.705
from our sister lab and that's
New York. So, take it up.

00:00:18.706 --> 00:00:21.210
&gt;&gt; Great. Okay. So, now

00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:23.015
that Nicole has told
you all to sit down,

00:00:23.016 --> 00:00:25.425
I want you all to stand back up.

00:00:25.426 --> 00:00:29.995
Great. Okay. Now I want you
to either stamp your feet,

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:32.074
jump up and down or stretch.

00:00:32.075 --> 00:00:39.165
Okay. Perfect. Now you can
sit back down. All right.

00:00:39.166 --> 00:00:40.910
So, I want this to be

00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:44.030
an interactive panel and I
want people to ask questions,

00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:46.780
and so I wanted to make sure
you're all paying attention.

00:00:46.781 --> 00:00:49.485
Good. All right.

00:00:49.486 --> 00:00:51.700
So, as Nicole said, I'm Hanna.

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:53.295
For those of you
who don't know me,

00:00:53.296 --> 00:00:55.370
I'm a principal researcher in

00:00:55.371 --> 00:00:57.860
Microsoft Research New York and

00:00:57.861 --> 00:01:00.820
an adjunct professor
at UMass Amherst.

00:01:00.821 --> 00:01:03.865
My background is in
Machine Learning,

00:01:03.866 --> 00:01:07.550
and specifically I study
Machine Learning and society.

00:01:07.551 --> 00:01:11.120
So, I both use Machine Learning
to study society,

00:01:11.121 --> 00:01:13.130
and I study the effect that

00:01:13.131 --> 00:01:15.690
Machine Learning has on society,

00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:18.020
as Machine Learning
and AI are becoming

00:01:18.021 --> 00:01:20.325
much more common and widespread.

00:01:20.326 --> 00:01:22.160
Over the past few years,

00:01:22.161 --> 00:01:23.990
I've been concentrating
specifically

00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:25.535
on issues of fairness,

00:01:25.536 --> 00:01:29.210
accountability,
transparency and ethics as

00:01:29.211 --> 00:01:31.339
they relate to AI and
Machine Learning.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.890
Indeed, that is the topic

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:35.405
of today's panel, unsurprisingly.

00:01:35.406 --> 00:01:39.710
I think it's a really
important and timely topic.

00:01:39.711 --> 00:01:41.330
So, I'm really glad
that we're having

00:01:41.331 --> 00:01:43.760
this panel discussion
on this here today.

00:01:43.761 --> 00:01:45.780
So, at this point,

00:01:45.781 --> 00:01:47.360
it's pretty clear that we're kind

00:01:47.361 --> 00:01:49.070
of living in the age of AI.

00:01:49.071 --> 00:01:51.080
Machine Learning is everywhere

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:52.490
and at least for the moment,

00:01:52.491 --> 00:01:55.900
it looks like it's here to
stay and this is great.

00:01:55.901 --> 00:01:58.265
This is great for
researchers like me.

00:01:58.266 --> 00:02:01.060
This is great for
companies like Microsoft.

00:02:01.061 --> 00:02:03.290
But at the same time,
we're also seeing that

00:02:03.291 --> 00:02:06.230
these kinds of
opportunities also raise

00:02:06.231 --> 00:02:08.810
new challenges and challenges
that have received

00:02:08.811 --> 00:02:11.625
a lot of attention in
the media recently.

00:02:11.626 --> 00:02:13.910
I've really highlighted
how important it

00:02:13.911 --> 00:02:16.000
is that we get AI right,

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:18.770
that we don't let
AI discriminate or

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:22.369
disadvantage previously
disadvantaged groups.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.380
So, how many of you have
seen one of these stories?

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.960
Great, so pretty much everyone.

00:02:29.961 --> 00:02:31.450
That's what I was hoping for.

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:33.790
Good. So, then you'll
know that this is

00:02:33.791 --> 00:02:36.895
a really complicated
and fraught landscape

00:02:36.896 --> 00:02:39.675
with a lot of different
key stakeholders,

00:02:39.676 --> 00:02:42.220
ranging from yeah
sure researchers but

00:02:42.221 --> 00:02:45.030
companies like Microsoft,
the government,

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:48.095
civil rights and
civil liberties organizations,

00:02:48.096 --> 00:02:51.520
and of course the people who
use these technologies and

00:02:51.521 --> 00:02:55.270
who are affected by these
technologies in their lives.

00:02:55.271 --> 00:02:58.510
So, we haven't really
accomplished group of panelists

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:01.645
here today: Timnit Gebru,,
Debbie Hellman,

00:03:01.646 --> 00:03:04.570
Adam Kalai and Mira Lane
and they're going to

00:03:04.571 --> 00:03:07.840
explore this landscape
here today and in turn,

00:03:07.841 --> 00:03:09.580
I hope that you'll walk away from

00:03:09.581 --> 00:03:11.809
this panel with
a deeper understanding

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.400
of why these issues are so

00:03:13.401 --> 00:03:15.990
important, why
they're non-trivial,

00:03:15.991 --> 00:03:18.920
why they really get at
the core of the relationship

00:03:18.921 --> 00:03:21.830
between technology
and society and

00:03:21.831 --> 00:03:24.800
why it's important that we
take them really seriously

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:28.390
even when there are
no obvious right answers.

00:03:28.391 --> 00:03:31.485
So, here's how this
is going to go.

00:03:31.486 --> 00:03:35.500
I am going to start by briefly
introducing the panelists,

00:03:35.501 --> 00:03:38.330
and then I'm going to
ask each panelist to

00:03:38.331 --> 00:03:41.090
talk for a few minutes
about themselves,

00:03:41.091 --> 00:03:42.830
their own work and how they

00:03:42.831 --> 00:03:45.030
work relates to the
topics of fairness,

00:03:45.031 --> 00:03:48.025
accountability,
transparency and ethics.

00:03:48.026 --> 00:03:49.520
Then what I'm gonna
do is I'm going

00:03:49.521 --> 00:03:50.570
to ask them a series of

00:03:50.571 --> 00:03:52.975
questions to get
the discussion going,

00:03:52.976 --> 00:03:55.340
and after that, I'm going
to hand the floor over to

00:03:55.341 --> 00:03:58.040
you and this is really
your opportunity.

00:03:58.041 --> 00:04:00.440
I'm sure many of you
have questions about

00:04:00.441 --> 00:04:03.700
these topics that you really
want to know the answers to.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.175
This is your opportunity
to ask them.

00:04:06.176 --> 00:04:09.735
Okay. If at anytime you
want to ask a question,

00:04:09.736 --> 00:04:11.930
either while the panelists
are talking about

00:04:11.931 --> 00:04:14.590
their own work or while
I'm asking questions,

00:04:14.591 --> 00:04:16.100
you should feel free to do so.

00:04:16.101 --> 00:04:18.200
Just raise your hand
and I will wave at

00:04:18.201 --> 00:04:21.100
Nicole and she will
run a mic over to you.

00:04:21.101 --> 00:04:26.685
Okay. Wonderful.
Great. So, with that,

00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:28.470
I'm going to introduce
our panelists.

00:04:28.471 --> 00:04:32.120
Okay. So Timnit Gebru just
finished her post-doc in

00:04:32.121 --> 00:04:34.710
the faith group at
Microsoft Research, New York

00:04:34.711 --> 00:04:36.710
and we miss her a lot.

00:04:36.711 --> 00:04:39.260
She is now a research
scientist in

00:04:39.261 --> 00:04:40.565
the ethical AI team at

00:04:40.566 --> 00:04:43.160
Google AI and she's
currently studying

00:04:43.161 --> 00:04:45.424
the ethical
considerations underlying

00:04:45.425 --> 00:04:47.330
any data mining project

00:04:47.331 --> 00:04:49.850
and methods of auditing
and mitigating

00:04:49.851 --> 00:04:52.940
bias in socio-technical systems.

00:04:52.941 --> 00:04:55.640
As a co-founder of
the group Black in AI,

00:04:55.641 --> 00:04:58.010
she works to
both increase diversity

00:04:58.011 --> 00:04:59.870
in the field and to reduce

00:04:59.871 --> 00:05:02.180
the negative impacts
of racial bias in

00:05:02.181 --> 00:05:03.800
training data used for

00:05:03.801 --> 00:05:06.934
human-centric
machine-learning models.

00:05:06.935 --> 00:05:11.670
Great. So, Debbie
Hellman is a D. Lurton

00:05:11.671 --> 00:05:13.680
Massee Professor of Law in

00:05:13.681 --> 00:05:16.330
the University of Virginia,
School of Law.

00:05:16.331 --> 00:05:18.925
Her work focuses
on discrimination,

00:05:18.926 --> 00:05:22.280
both from a philosophical
and a legal perspective.

00:05:22.281 --> 00:05:25.210
She's particularly
interested in the questions,

00:05:25.211 --> 00:05:28.855
what is discrimination
and what makes it wrong?

00:05:28.856 --> 00:05:30.650
In fact, she wrote a book called

00:05:30.651 --> 00:05:32.810
When is Discrimination Wrong in

00:05:32.811 --> 00:05:35.060
2008 and has continued to work

00:05:35.061 --> 00:05:37.950
on these topics in
the years since.

00:05:37.951 --> 00:05:41.195
She's also new to
AI and therefore

00:05:41.196 --> 00:05:42.590
wants you to know
that you shouldn't

00:05:42.591 --> 00:05:45.270
ask her anything super technical.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:52.600
Okay. Adam. Adam Kalai

00:05:52.601 --> 00:05:55.550
is a very serious
principal researcher

00:05:55.551 --> 00:05:56.925
in Microsoft Research,

00:05:56.926 --> 00:05:59.180
New England, although
he is better

00:05:59.181 --> 00:06:02.850
known as the husband
of Yael Kalai.

00:06:02.851 --> 00:06:07.360
Adam has had over
50 papers rejected,

00:06:07.361 --> 00:06:10.100
counting multiple
submissions, multiple

00:06:10.101 --> 00:06:13.005
times in top
computer science venues,

00:06:13.006 --> 00:06:16.015
only the best for Adam.

00:06:16.016 --> 00:06:19.925
The last time I moderated
a panel that Adam was on,

00:06:19.926 --> 00:06:22.235
he made a fool of me.

00:06:22.236 --> 00:06:25.120
I'm going to get my revenge,

00:06:25.121 --> 00:06:26.640
but I've decided that I won't

00:06:26.641 --> 00:06:28.260
tell him when this
is going to happen.

00:06:28.261 --> 00:06:30.450
It may be in today's panel,

00:06:30.451 --> 00:06:33.615
it may be in the next panel
that I moderate that he's on.

00:06:33.616 --> 00:06:35.890
We will see.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:40.140
Our final panelist is Mira Lane,

00:06:40.141 --> 00:06:42.530
who is the partner
director of ethics and

00:06:42.531 --> 00:06:46.040
society within Cloud
and AI at Microsoft.

00:06:46.041 --> 00:06:48.080
She has a background in art,

00:06:48.081 --> 00:06:51.245
computer science and
mathematics and she holds

00:06:51.246 --> 00:06:52.820
numerous patents across

00:06:52.821 --> 00:06:55.650
platforms and
collaborative interfaces.

00:06:55.651 --> 00:06:58.130
Mira's team is
responsible for guiding

00:06:58.131 --> 00:07:01.705
technical and experience
innovation towards ethical,

00:07:01.706 --> 00:07:04.670
responsible and
sustainable outcomes.

00:07:04.671 --> 00:07:08.670
So, please join me in
welcoming our panelists.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:13.780
&gt;&gt; Okay. So now we're going

00:07:13.781 --> 00:07:15.370
to have the panelists each talk

00:07:15.371 --> 00:07:16.915
a little bit about their work

00:07:16.916 --> 00:07:18.510
and how it relates to fairness,

00:07:18.511 --> 00:07:21.170
accountability,
transparency, and ethics.

00:07:21.171 --> 00:07:22.830
I think maybe what we'll do since

00:07:22.831 --> 00:07:24.465
I started with Timney before,

00:07:24.466 --> 00:07:26.200
I will start with Mary this time.

00:07:26.201 --> 00:07:29.390
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, as
Hannah was saying,

00:07:29.391 --> 00:07:32.120
I'm part of the Engineering
team at Microsoft.

00:07:32.121 --> 00:07:34.330
I'm not part of
Microsoft Research.

00:07:34.331 --> 00:07:35.850
But what you've heard over

00:07:35.851 --> 00:07:37.255
and over again and
all of the panels,

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:39.580
is a need for a bridge between

00:07:39.581 --> 00:07:42.320
research and what's happening
on the applied side.

00:07:42.321 --> 00:07:44.705
So, my team acts as a bridge.

00:07:44.706 --> 00:07:46.530
What we do, is we take ideas

00:07:46.531 --> 00:07:48.290
and policies that are happening

00:07:48.291 --> 00:07:49.890
in research and across

00:07:49.891 --> 00:07:52.080
Microsoft and then we
look at those and say,

00:07:52.081 --> 00:07:53.510
"How do we bring these into

00:07:53.511 --> 00:07:55.400
an engineering
context and how do we

00:07:55.401 --> 00:07:57.570
apply these so that
thousands of engineers,

00:07:57.571 --> 00:07:59.340
designers, product thinkers,

00:07:59.341 --> 00:08:01.230
sales folks can then

00:08:01.231 --> 00:08:03.510
do responsible things
with technology."

00:08:03.511 --> 00:08:05.050
This involves everything from

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:06.665
looking at how we collect data,

00:08:06.666 --> 00:08:08.330
how we do scenario planning,

00:08:08.331 --> 00:08:11.265
how we look at impacts and
consequences of our work,

00:08:11.266 --> 00:08:12.970
how we actually develop and where

00:08:12.971 --> 00:08:14.825
we set benchmarks in our code,

00:08:14.826 --> 00:08:15.990
and then how we maintain

00:08:15.991 --> 00:08:18.945
our systems once they've
gone out the door.

00:08:18.946 --> 00:08:21.640
So, this is a new type
of model in the company.

00:08:21.641 --> 00:08:24.045
I don't think anything
like this quite exists,

00:08:24.046 --> 00:08:25.500
and so, it's an experiment.

00:08:25.501 --> 00:08:27.345
We're innovating and
we're trying new things,

00:08:27.346 --> 00:08:28.810
but it's a fairly big investment.

00:08:28.811 --> 00:08:30.700
There's about 20 people
working on this.

00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:32.660
It's a multidisciplinary group.

00:08:32.661 --> 00:08:34.745
There are social
psychologists on the team,

00:08:34.746 --> 00:08:37.040
there are developers, designers,

00:08:37.041 --> 00:08:40.005
storytellers,
creative coders and, so,

00:08:40.006 --> 00:08:42.280
we're playing around
with this space and

00:08:42.281 --> 00:08:44.270
we're trying to bring
responsible thinking

00:08:44.271 --> 00:08:46.095
and responsible innovation into

00:08:46.096 --> 00:08:48.310
a complex and gnarly space,

00:08:48.311 --> 00:08:50.175
where we look at
speech technologies,

00:08:50.176 --> 00:08:51.970
facial recognition, we do

00:08:51.971 --> 00:08:54.405
things around virtual
reality and HoloLens.

00:08:54.406 --> 00:08:55.870
So, a lot of
the gnarly things that

00:08:55.871 --> 00:08:57.140
you tend to see in the news,

00:08:57.141 --> 00:08:59.590
are in this group
that I'm working on.

00:08:59.591 --> 00:09:00.480
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:09:00.481 --> 00:09:01.075
&gt;&gt; You are welcome.

00:09:01.076 --> 00:09:01.885
&gt;&gt; All right. Adam.

00:09:01.886 --> 00:09:04.820
&gt;&gt; I am a Machine Learning
AI Researcher

00:09:04.821 --> 00:09:06.400
and you can think of me as

00:09:06.401 --> 00:09:08.925
the naive user of AI
Machine Learning that would have

00:09:08.926 --> 00:09:12.160
naturally made algorithms
that discriminate except,

00:09:12.161 --> 00:09:14.750
that I'm in this lab surrounded
by people like this and

00:09:14.751 --> 00:09:16.240
many other people in our lab and

00:09:16.241 --> 00:09:18.260
then they made us
think before, Oh gee!

00:09:18.261 --> 00:09:19.845
Maybe we should think
a little bit about what

00:09:19.846 --> 00:09:22.280
these technologies will
do and then we started

00:09:22.281 --> 00:09:23.350
doing that and then covering

00:09:23.351 --> 00:09:26.650
various vices that
we sourced in words,

00:09:26.651 --> 00:09:28.250
that you might see gender bias or

00:09:28.251 --> 00:09:30.310
racial biases and what effects

00:09:30.311 --> 00:09:32.160
that they will have on
various algorithms we design.

00:09:32.161 --> 00:09:33.730
So, we are also
thinking about how we

00:09:33.731 --> 00:09:36.810
might deal with that.

00:09:36.811 --> 00:09:39.465
&gt;&gt; Great. Thank you Adam. Okay.

00:09:39.466 --> 00:09:40.720
&gt;&gt; Okay, I hope I'm
not going to talk for

00:09:40.721 --> 00:09:41.950
too long because
those were shorter.

00:09:41.951 --> 00:09:43.290
But I thought I'd tell you about

00:09:43.291 --> 00:09:45.260
a paper I'm working
on now just because I

00:09:45.261 --> 00:09:46.900
always think it's interesting
to hear what people are

00:09:46.901 --> 00:09:48.930
working on and because
it's a great resource.

00:09:48.931 --> 00:09:50.110
It's a little bit selfish,

00:09:50.111 --> 00:09:51.130
a great resource for me.

00:09:51.131 --> 00:09:53.080
In case you guys have some input,

00:09:53.081 --> 00:09:54.950
I would love to hear
it because as I said,

00:09:54.951 --> 00:09:56.625
I'm a bit of an interloper here.

00:09:56.626 --> 00:09:58.760
So I'm working on a paper
that has to do with

00:09:58.761 --> 00:10:01.600
the controversy about the
COMPAS Risk Assessment Tool,

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.155
which I imagine most of
you are familiar with.

00:10:04.156 --> 00:10:06.800
I see most heads nodding
but just briefly,

00:10:06.801 --> 00:10:09.620
it's a risk
assessment tool that's

00:10:09.621 --> 00:10:12.530
used to determine who should
get out of prison early,

00:10:12.531 --> 00:10:14.420
on parole, it's also
used in bail settings

00:10:14.421 --> 00:10:16.480
in many many states
around the country.

00:10:16.481 --> 00:10:18.700
And there was a big brouhaha

00:10:18.701 --> 00:10:20.800
after an expose by ProPublica,

00:10:20.801 --> 00:10:23.755
which alleged that the tool

00:10:23.756 --> 00:10:26.080
discriminated between blacks and

00:10:26.081 --> 00:10:28.485
whites and didn't
treat them fairly.

00:10:28.486 --> 00:10:30.870
The response from the company

00:10:30.871 --> 00:10:32.865
that makes COMPAS
which is Northpointe,

00:10:32.866 --> 00:10:34.530
was to say, "That's not true,

00:10:34.531 --> 00:10:38.690
the tool does treat blacks
and whites fairly or doesn't

00:10:38.691 --> 00:10:40.605
discriminate between
blacks and whites,"

00:10:40.606 --> 00:10:43.270
and the up short of
that debate was that,

00:10:43.271 --> 00:10:45.405
they were focusing on
different measures.

00:10:45.406 --> 00:10:47.780
That, Northpointe was focusing on

00:10:47.781 --> 00:10:50.130
one measure for assessing
whether blacks and

00:10:50.131 --> 00:10:53.035
whites are treated equally and

00:10:53.036 --> 00:10:55.350
ProPublica and
the critics of COMPAS

00:10:55.351 --> 00:10:57.660
were focusing on another measure.

00:10:57.661 --> 00:11:00.350
That led to then a
series of papers by

00:11:00.351 --> 00:11:03.675
people like you all
that showed that,

00:11:03.676 --> 00:11:06.540
in many many contexts,

00:11:06.541 --> 00:11:08.215
in fact in most contexts,

00:11:08.216 --> 00:11:10.405
it would be impossible to

00:11:10.406 --> 00:11:12.700
equalize with regard
to both measures.

00:11:12.701 --> 00:11:14.080
That is, you couldn't
treat blacks and

00:11:14.081 --> 00:11:15.920
whites equally with
regard to the measure

00:11:15.921 --> 00:11:18.785
that Northpointe was focusing
on and at the same time

00:11:18.786 --> 00:11:20.810
with regard to the measure

00:11:20.811 --> 00:11:23.200
that ProPublica was focusing on.

00:11:23.201 --> 00:11:24.845
Thus leading to the question,

00:11:24.846 --> 00:11:28.165
which one should
we prefer and why?

00:11:28.166 --> 00:11:29.440
In a second series of

00:11:29.441 --> 00:11:32.045
papers that looked
at that question,

00:11:32.046 --> 00:11:35.280
that second generation
question was posed as,

00:11:35.281 --> 00:11:40.210
which measure of fairness
should we prefer and why?

00:11:40.211 --> 00:11:43.495
Where I want to
intercede in this debate

00:11:43.496 --> 00:11:46.445
is to say that there's something
wrong with that framing,

00:11:46.446 --> 00:11:50.005
that they're not both
measures of fairness,

00:11:50.006 --> 00:11:51.955
and so, we're not comparing

00:11:51.956 --> 00:11:53.700
two things in the same domain.

00:11:53.701 --> 00:11:55.320
That one of the measures
is a measure of

00:11:55.321 --> 00:11:57.275
fairness and the other measure

00:11:57.276 --> 00:11:59.110
maybe has some implications for

00:11:59.111 --> 00:12:01.645
fairness but isn't itself
a measure of fairness.

00:12:01.646 --> 00:12:04.305
So just if I can say
a few more words,

00:12:04.306 --> 00:12:06.000
the two measures, the one that

00:12:06.001 --> 00:12:07.890
Northpointe was focusing on was,

00:12:07.891 --> 00:12:09.910
if two people get a risk score

00:12:09.911 --> 00:12:12.490
of let's say eight out of 10,

00:12:12.491 --> 00:12:14.200
that a black person and

00:12:14.201 --> 00:12:15.875
a white person who
both get a score of

00:12:15.876 --> 00:12:19.190
eight are equally
likely to recidivate.

00:12:19.191 --> 00:12:21.540
That is, eight out of
10 people who get that score,

00:12:21.541 --> 00:12:22.990
will in fact recidivate.

00:12:22.991 --> 00:12:26.480
So the score was equally
predictive for both groups.

00:12:26.481 --> 00:12:29.815
So it was an equal
predictive accuracy.

00:12:29.816 --> 00:12:35.005
That was true for
for the COMPAS tool.

00:12:35.006 --> 00:12:39.030
The measure that
ProPublica was focused on,

00:12:39.031 --> 00:12:41.175
was not asking if
you got a score,

00:12:41.176 --> 00:12:43.185
what was the likelihood
that you would recidivate,

00:12:43.186 --> 00:12:44.710
was probably asking the question

00:12:44.711 --> 00:12:46.560
in a different kind
of way was saying,

00:12:46.561 --> 00:12:50.720
"If you are someone who was
at low risk for recidivating,

00:12:50.721 --> 00:12:52.845
what's the likelihood
that the tool would

00:12:52.846 --> 00:12:56.000
accurately characterize
you as low risk?"

00:12:56.001 --> 00:12:59.340
There, there was
a big difference between

00:12:59.341 --> 00:13:01.700
how a low-risk black and

00:13:01.701 --> 00:13:04.775
a low-risk white person were
characterized by the tool.

00:13:04.776 --> 00:13:08.640
A low-risk black person
was much more likely to be

00:13:08.641 --> 00:13:10.555
mis-characterized by the tool

00:13:10.556 --> 00:13:12.420
than a low-risk white person.

00:13:12.421 --> 00:13:16.075
So, another way of

00:13:16.076 --> 00:13:18.360
characterizing this
would be to say that,

00:13:18.361 --> 00:13:20.870
the scores were equally accurate,

00:13:20.871 --> 00:13:23.060
they had equal predictive
accuracy but of course they

00:13:23.061 --> 00:13:25.524
weren't perfect and when
there were mistakes,

00:13:25.525 --> 00:13:27.790
the mistakes went in
different directions.

00:13:27.791 --> 00:13:29.410
One had more false positives and

00:13:29.411 --> 00:13:31.690
the other more more
false negatives.

00:13:31.691 --> 00:13:32.890
So then the question is,

00:13:32.891 --> 00:13:34.675
which measure do we care about,

00:13:34.676 --> 00:13:39.470
equal predictive accuracy or
equalizing the error rates?

00:13:39.471 --> 00:13:42.560
The false positives and
false negative rates.

00:13:42.561 --> 00:13:45.260
What I'm arguing in this paper,

00:13:45.261 --> 00:13:47.760
which I'm happy to talk to
you more about in the Q

00:13:47.761 --> 00:13:50.835
and A or in coffee, is that,

00:13:50.836 --> 00:13:54.260
the measure that looked at
equalizing the error rates,

00:13:54.261 --> 00:13:57.055
is a measure of fairness
because it's looking at,

00:13:57.056 --> 00:14:00.030
what's the burden of mistakes.

00:14:00.031 --> 00:14:02.380
How are, is, are the burdens of

00:14:02.381 --> 00:14:05.565
mistakes falling equally
on the two groups?

00:14:05.566 --> 00:14:08.650
So it relates to the way
in which we treat people.

00:14:08.651 --> 00:14:11.300
Whereas equal
predictive accuracy is

00:14:11.301 --> 00:14:13.955
about what we believe
about people.

00:14:13.956 --> 00:14:17.920
That is, it's about
belief versus action.

00:14:17.921 --> 00:14:19.440
That is, if the scores don't

00:14:19.441 --> 00:14:21.420
have equal predictive accuracy,

00:14:21.421 --> 00:14:22.935
a score of eight will mean

00:14:22.936 --> 00:14:24.770
something different
for a black person

00:14:24.771 --> 00:14:26.090
versus a white person if we give

00:14:26.091 --> 00:14:28.250
up on predictive accuracy.

00:14:28.251 --> 00:14:30.350
Therefore, I won't
know what to believe

00:14:30.351 --> 00:14:32.300
about a person who
has a score of eight,

00:14:32.301 --> 00:14:34.670
I won't know what to believe
about what that means,

00:14:34.671 --> 00:14:37.175
about the likelihood that
he or she will recidivate.

00:14:37.176 --> 00:14:39.710
But a score that
relates to what I ought

00:14:39.711 --> 00:14:42.620
to believe versus
what I ought to do,

00:14:42.621 --> 00:14:46.295
is not related to fairness
in any straightforward way.

00:14:46.296 --> 00:14:51.320
That's the basic conceptual
point of the paper is that,

00:14:51.321 --> 00:14:53.600
predictive accuracy
relates to belief,

00:14:53.601 --> 00:14:55.820
error rate balance
relates to action,

00:14:55.821 --> 00:14:57.200
and therefore it's more naturally

00:14:57.201 --> 00:14:59.700
a fairness space concern.

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:04.960
&gt;&gt; I'm glad you brought
up the compass dataset

00:15:04.961 --> 00:15:08.760
because I studied
Computer Vision,

00:15:08.761 --> 00:15:10.750
and the only interests I

00:15:10.751 --> 00:15:13.285
had was in studying
Computer Vision.

00:15:13.286 --> 00:15:15.850
But towards the end of my PhD,

00:15:15.851 --> 00:15:18.790
I read that ProPublica article
and I was terrified.

00:15:18.791 --> 00:15:21.550
So, that's how I
started to get more

00:15:21.551 --> 00:15:24.445
interested in these questions
of issues and fairness.

00:15:24.446 --> 00:15:26.860
So, my PhD was in using

00:15:26.861 --> 00:15:29.635
Computer Vision to do
visual data mining.

00:15:29.636 --> 00:15:32.890
So, for example, using images
to predict things about

00:15:32.891 --> 00:15:36.520
the society and that
naturally has certain biases,

00:15:36.521 --> 00:15:39.025
the kinds of biases
that we might discuss.

00:15:39.026 --> 00:15:41.695
So, that's when I
realized even my own work

00:15:41.696 --> 00:15:45.445
suffers from these
issues of fairness.

00:15:45.446 --> 00:15:49.029
So, I then met Joy Bolognini,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:52.420
who is advisee of

00:15:52.421 --> 00:15:55.780
Ethan who was moderating
the prior panel and

00:15:55.781 --> 00:15:58.630
we worked on a paper
showing that there are

00:15:58.631 --> 00:16:02.995
disparities in the commercial
face recognition systems,

00:16:02.996 --> 00:16:05.020
and how accurate they

00:16:05.021 --> 00:16:06.520
are for different
subgroups of people.

00:16:06.521 --> 00:16:08.490
So, we looked at skin types.

00:16:08.491 --> 00:16:10.720
We used a dermatology
classification system

00:16:10.721 --> 00:16:13.825
for skin tone and gender.

00:16:13.826 --> 00:16:17.725
In addition to my work in
Computer Vision and this work,

00:16:17.726 --> 00:16:22.220
I view what's for me equally
important is to make

00:16:22.221 --> 00:16:23.980
sure that the voices
of the people who are

00:16:23.981 --> 00:16:26.740
unfairly affected by these
systems are amplified.

00:16:26.741 --> 00:16:28.420
So, for example, because

00:16:28.421 --> 00:16:30.025
diversity gives you
domain knowledge.

00:16:30.026 --> 00:16:31.690
So, Joy Bolongini, she's

00:16:31.691 --> 00:16:33.880
a much darker skinned
Black woman and

00:16:33.881 --> 00:16:36.430
so the reason that
she came up with

00:16:36.431 --> 00:16:37.930
this idea of using

00:16:37.931 --> 00:16:40.120
Fitzpatrick skin type
rather than race,

00:16:40.121 --> 00:16:41.650
which a lot of people had

00:16:41.651 --> 00:16:43.870
done this kind of announces with

00:16:43.871 --> 00:16:46.360
race is because of
her domain knowledge

00:16:46.361 --> 00:16:48.910
about colors and about
a whole bunch of other things.

00:16:48.911 --> 00:16:50.890
So, I think, equally
important is to

00:16:50.891 --> 00:16:52.030
make sure that the people who

00:16:52.031 --> 00:16:53.520
bring this domain knowledge,

00:16:53.521 --> 00:16:56.780
that their voices are amplified.

00:16:56.781 --> 00:16:59.550
&gt;&gt; Great. Thanks everyone.

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:01.780
Okay. So, I'm going to start with

00:17:01.781 --> 00:17:05.010
some questions and
the first question which I

00:17:05.011 --> 00:17:06.485
want to ask really is

00:17:06.486 --> 00:17:11.245
a overview question and
stage setting question is,

00:17:11.246 --> 00:17:13.670
what are the different
ways that one

00:17:13.671 --> 00:17:16.185
might think about
whether a decision or

00:17:16.186 --> 00:17:19.860
output obtained via
an AI based system

00:17:19.861 --> 00:17:22.490
treats people fairly or not?

00:17:22.491 --> 00:17:24.950
I'm going to kick that question
over to you Debbie

00:17:24.951 --> 00:17:27.450
because of your background
in discrimination.

00:17:27.451 --> 00:17:29.150
&gt;&gt; So, the first point I

00:17:29.151 --> 00:17:31.425
would emphasize
about that is that

00:17:31.426 --> 00:17:33.550
there are different
ways of answering

00:17:33.551 --> 00:17:35.740
that question and people
disagree about that,

00:17:35.741 --> 00:17:40.240
and so that's what's
interesting, anyway about it.

00:17:40.241 --> 00:17:41.790
So, obviously, one way

00:17:41.791 --> 00:17:44.025
that people think about
what fairness is,

00:17:44.026 --> 00:17:46.680
is the idea of
treating like cases

00:17:46.681 --> 00:17:49.860
alike and I think many people
strive for that idea.

00:17:49.861 --> 00:17:51.390
I don't think that's

00:17:51.391 --> 00:17:53.150
the only way of
thinking about it and I

00:17:53.151 --> 00:17:56.635
think we make a mistake if
we emphasize that too much,

00:17:56.636 --> 00:17:58.210
but that is obviously one way of

00:17:58.211 --> 00:18:00.180
thinking about what fairness is,

00:18:00.181 --> 00:18:01.555
is treating like cases alike.

00:18:01.556 --> 00:18:03.150
Another way would be treating

00:18:03.151 --> 00:18:04.630
everybody literally the same.

00:18:04.631 --> 00:18:06.300
That's not that plausible in

00:18:06.301 --> 00:18:08.400
most situations but another idea.

00:18:08.401 --> 00:18:11.140
A third way is one that
you mentioned before,

00:18:11.141 --> 00:18:12.865
which is making sure not to

00:18:12.866 --> 00:18:15.870
disadvantage already
disadvantaged groups.

00:18:15.871 --> 00:18:18.265
A fourth way, which is,

00:18:18.266 --> 00:18:21.645
we see dominant in
legal discourse is just

00:18:21.646 --> 00:18:25.580
avoiding the use of certain
traits or classifications.

00:18:25.581 --> 00:18:27.765
That's another conception.

00:18:27.766 --> 00:18:31.765
Then a fifth one might be
not doing things that are

00:18:31.766 --> 00:18:35.580
demeaning in some way and that
is not an exhaustive list,

00:18:35.581 --> 00:18:38.800
but just gives you
a flavor of possibilities.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:41.620
&gt;&gt; Do any of the rest of you

00:18:41.621 --> 00:18:43.205
have anything to add to that?

00:18:43.206 --> 00:18:45.760
&gt;&gt; Well, I think
one of the things

00:18:45.761 --> 00:18:48.955
to add to that is if
we look back into,

00:18:48.956 --> 00:18:50.750
again the engineering system,

00:18:50.751 --> 00:18:52.420
having a robust set of

00:18:52.421 --> 00:18:54.910
test cases that we can
then go and apply against

00:18:54.911 --> 00:18:57.640
the systems to say
what kinds of discrimination

00:18:57.641 --> 00:19:00.620
might be happening in
the system, is very important.

00:19:00.621 --> 00:19:02.570
Because one of
the things we don't do

00:19:02.571 --> 00:19:04.480
right now is actually separate

00:19:04.481 --> 00:19:05.960
out those subgroups and

00:19:05.961 --> 00:19:08.775
the categories of people that
might be using a system.

00:19:08.776 --> 00:19:10.140
It's something that
we're trying to

00:19:10.141 --> 00:19:11.710
build into the new way that we

00:19:11.711 --> 00:19:14.770
design advanced technologies
and look at: Well,

00:19:14.771 --> 00:19:17.870
we know that the system is
going to impact these classes

00:19:17.871 --> 00:19:19.460
of people and we know that some

00:19:19.461 --> 00:19:21.180
of these are
vulnerable audiences,

00:19:21.181 --> 00:19:24.555
and how do we proactively go and

00:19:24.556 --> 00:19:26.540
investigate the system
to see how they're

00:19:26.541 --> 00:19:28.670
performing against
certain subgroups?

00:19:28.671 --> 00:19:32.160
And then, go in and set
benchmarks and say, okay,

00:19:32.161 --> 00:19:35.025
your code cannot go out
the door if it doesn't meet

00:19:35.026 --> 00:19:36.710
a certain criteria across

00:19:36.711 --> 00:19:38.150
all of these different subgroups.

00:19:38.151 --> 00:19:39.960
There's a lot of work
that we need to do

00:19:39.961 --> 00:19:41.770
to even get there but that's how

00:19:41.771 --> 00:19:44.160
I would take what you're
saying and translate it

00:19:44.161 --> 00:19:47.020
into getting the system
to perform a certain way.

00:19:47.021 --> 00:19:49.960
Because the thing that
I worry about the most,

00:19:49.961 --> 00:19:52.910
is that we end up
talking about it a lot,

00:19:52.911 --> 00:19:55.560
but then we don't go and
change the system in

00:19:55.561 --> 00:19:59.095
such a way that it
performs in a way that is

00:19:59.096 --> 00:20:02.625
fair or it puts fairness
into the system

00:20:02.626 --> 00:20:04.580
and I don't think

00:20:04.581 --> 00:20:06.550
enough work is happening
in that space right now.

00:20:06.551 --> 00:20:09.590
Which is why it's so important
to me to go in and be

00:20:09.591 --> 00:20:11.550
that bridge between
all the great work that you guys

00:20:11.551 --> 00:20:13.520
are doing and then the engineers,

00:20:13.521 --> 00:20:15.260
who are not necessarily
thinking about

00:20:15.261 --> 00:20:18.250
this problem space in
the same way that this group is.

00:20:18.251 --> 00:20:19.545
&gt;&gt; Can I just? Go.

00:20:19.546 --> 00:20:20.910
&gt;&gt; For data sheets,

00:20:20.911 --> 00:20:22.560
for data sets that
paper that we wrote.

00:20:22.561 --> 00:20:23.490
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:20:23.491 --> 00:20:25.710
&gt;&gt; Another one I
just score called

00:20:25.711 --> 00:20:28.050
model cards from
model performance,

00:20:28.051 --> 00:20:30.275
which is trying to basically say,

00:20:30.276 --> 00:20:31.945
if you're going to, let's say,

00:20:31.946 --> 00:20:34.285
let's take a
face recognition system.

00:20:34.286 --> 00:20:38.590
Before you have a pre-trained
model or an API available,

00:20:38.591 --> 00:20:41.755
how about you tell us some of
the tests that you've done,

00:20:41.756 --> 00:20:44.290
some of the false positive rates
or false negative rates,

00:20:44.291 --> 00:20:45.910
or we have a whole host of tests,

00:20:45.911 --> 00:20:48.710
but I'm just making a plan.

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:50.260
&gt;&gt; You were going to respond.

00:20:50.261 --> 00:20:52.270
&gt;&gt; What I was going to
say for data sheets,

00:20:52.271 --> 00:20:55.000
we've actually been
spending a lot of time.

00:20:55.001 --> 00:20:57.180
So, I'll give you

00:20:57.181 --> 00:20:58.320
the high-level view
and then maybe

00:20:58.321 --> 00:20:59.490
Hannah you can come in and say,

00:20:59.491 --> 00:21:02.590
"Mira there's a more robust way
to talk about it."

00:21:02.591 --> 00:21:05.160
But when I talk about
data sheets to our engineers,

00:21:05.161 --> 00:21:06.150
I say, "These are basically

00:21:06.151 --> 00:21:08.110
nutrition labels for datasets."

00:21:08.111 --> 00:21:09.550
So, when you look at a dataset,

00:21:09.551 --> 00:21:12.330
you understand the composition
of it, what's inside it?

00:21:12.331 --> 00:21:13.340
How it was collected?

00:21:13.341 --> 00:21:14.635
How can you use the data?

00:21:14.636 --> 00:21:16.360
So, that if you're
going and using

00:21:16.361 --> 00:21:19.015
this data in another model
that you might be training,

00:21:19.016 --> 00:21:22.870
you have a sense for what
that data can be used for and

00:21:22.871 --> 00:21:27.100
how that data's performing
against other things. Yeah.

00:21:27.101 --> 00:21:28.480
&gt;&gt; Tim, that you should.

00:21:28.481 --> 00:21:32.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, there's two parts.

00:21:32.021 --> 00:21:34.840
So, data sheets is for it
for datasets and I think,

00:21:34.841 --> 00:21:38.000
it would be helpful
for even when people

00:21:38.001 --> 00:21:41.255
are sharing datasets internally.

00:21:41.256 --> 00:21:43.330
Because a lot of time
somebody collects a dataset,

00:21:43.331 --> 00:21:47.420
uses it for one purpose
and another team gets

00:21:47.421 --> 00:21:49.670
that data to try to use it
for another purpose and then

00:21:49.671 --> 00:21:52.375
some information is lost
when you transfer it.

00:21:52.376 --> 00:21:54.170
You don't know what the

00:21:54.171 --> 00:21:57.310
original reason what the data
that was collected for,

00:21:57.311 --> 00:21:59.590
you don't know what
the legal considerations are,

00:21:59.591 --> 00:22:00.760
you don't know what

00:22:00.761 --> 00:22:04.150
the intended use cases
are, et cetera.

00:22:04.151 --> 00:22:05.350
So, we talk about

00:22:05.351 --> 00:22:06.910
fairness accountability
and transparency.

00:22:06.911 --> 00:22:09.790
I think this is low hanging
fruit in transparency.

00:22:09.791 --> 00:22:11.050
I think there's
a lot we can do in

00:22:11.051 --> 00:22:12.805
transparency that
we're not doing.

00:22:12.806 --> 00:22:15.520
So, another follow-up paper that

00:22:15.521 --> 00:22:18.340
I just mentioned is
a similar thing for models.

00:22:18.341 --> 00:22:21.040
Because when we were initially
writing data sheets,

00:22:21.041 --> 00:22:22.720
we were thinking
about datasets and

00:22:22.721 --> 00:22:25.060
models but it turned
out that it's actually

00:22:25.061 --> 00:22:30.099
very complicated to even just
do this idea for datasets,

00:22:30.100 --> 00:22:33.540
but then there's
a second part to it,

00:22:33.541 --> 00:22:36.405
which is when you're releasing
a model or an API like

00:22:36.406 --> 00:22:39.360
I've just talked about
a paper we wrote with Joy,

00:22:39.361 --> 00:22:40.360
where we were auditing

00:22:40.361 --> 00:22:42.735
commercial gender
classification systems.

00:22:42.736 --> 00:22:45.320
What kind of information
are you giving the user?

00:22:45.321 --> 00:22:47.650
Are you giving them
intended use cases?

00:22:47.651 --> 00:22:49.370
Are you giving them disclaimers?

00:22:49.371 --> 00:22:51.290
Like don't use this to

00:22:51.291 --> 00:22:53.940
decide whether someone
is a criminal or not.

00:22:53.941 --> 00:22:57.190
So, that's the follow-up
in terms of models.

00:22:57.191 --> 00:22:59.040
&gt;&gt; So, I just wanted
to push back on

00:22:59.041 --> 00:23:01.170
one thing that you said
if that's okay Mira.

00:23:01.171 --> 00:23:02.620
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:08.450
&gt;&gt; The idea that
you're going to audit,

00:23:08.451 --> 00:23:10.615
I don't know what word to
use but something like that,

00:23:10.616 --> 00:23:12.640
the systems, you still need to

00:23:12.641 --> 00:23:15.210
say what you're
auditing them for.

00:23:15.211 --> 00:23:16.790
So, if you're auditing them

00:23:16.791 --> 00:23:19.295
for the idea of treating
like cases alike

00:23:19.296 --> 00:23:21.680
versus auditing them
for the idea of

00:23:21.681 --> 00:23:24.850
disadvantaging previously
disadvantaged groups,

00:23:24.851 --> 00:23:26.890
those are often going to
yield very different answers.

00:23:26.891 --> 00:23:30.055
So, it doesn't have
to audit them for

00:23:30.056 --> 00:23:32.170
fairness but to the extent

00:23:32.171 --> 00:23:33.880
that we have different views
about what that is,

00:23:33.881 --> 00:23:35.605
that's going to yield
very different projects.

00:23:35.606 --> 00:23:37.770
&gt;&gt; There's one more fairness
that you didn't mention

00:23:37.771 --> 00:23:39.910
which is very widely,
very common one,

00:23:39.911 --> 00:23:41.150
which is just that if your system

00:23:41.151 --> 00:23:42.750
was trained with
any kind of data,

00:23:42.751 --> 00:23:44.775
then it's objective
and it's fair.

00:23:44.776 --> 00:23:47.970
&gt;&gt; With any kind of data?

00:23:47.971 --> 00:23:50.290
&gt;&gt; I don't believe it,
but I think in some ways,

00:23:50.291 --> 00:23:52.110
the datasheets work speaks to

00:23:52.111 --> 00:23:54.040
that and it says
to people, "Well,

00:23:54.041 --> 00:23:56.830
think about what data
you used," but it is

00:23:56.831 --> 00:23:58.240
a belief that a lot of
people have and that's

00:23:58.241 --> 00:23:59.700
one that we need to dispel,

00:23:59.701 --> 00:24:01.230
but many people find

00:24:01.231 --> 00:24:02.935
that intuitive or that's
their first reaction,

00:24:02.936 --> 00:24:04.780
"Oh this came with data.

00:24:04.781 --> 00:24:07.870
Therefore, it's probably fair."

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:10.650
Justifying things with data is

00:24:10.651 --> 00:24:13.765
a standard way to justify
making decisions,

00:24:13.766 --> 00:24:15.310
which isn't always
fair obviously.

00:24:15.311 --> 00:24:15.895
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:15.896 --> 00:24:18.180
&gt;&gt; So, I'm interested
in digging a little bit

00:24:18.181 --> 00:24:21.430
further into this idea of how

00:24:21.431 --> 00:24:24.150
each of you thinks
about fairness or

00:24:24.151 --> 00:24:27.850
even measures fairness when
it comes to AI based systems.

00:24:27.851 --> 00:24:29.340
As you said Debbie,

00:24:29.341 --> 00:24:31.110
there's a whole bunch
of different kinds of

00:24:31.111 --> 00:24:33.570
metrics or measures
that you can look at,

00:24:33.571 --> 00:24:35.460
there are various different
trade-offs between

00:24:35.461 --> 00:24:37.470
them and some types of unfairness

00:24:37.471 --> 00:24:39.570
might be just inherently
very difficult

00:24:39.571 --> 00:24:41.975
to quantify or to automate.

00:24:41.976 --> 00:24:45.070
So, I'm interested from
all of you to learn a

00:24:45.071 --> 00:24:48.370
little bit more about
which approaches,

00:24:48.371 --> 00:24:51.520
which measures, which
metrics do you use?

00:24:51.521 --> 00:24:55.140
How do you think about
testing for fairness?

00:24:56.080 --> 00:24:58.250
&gt;&gt; Actually, this
was a question I

00:24:58.251 --> 00:25:00.020
wanted to ask Debbie, too.

00:25:00.021 --> 00:25:03.260
So, the way I think about it
is I don't have anything to

00:25:03.261 --> 00:25:06.815
add to the metrics
that you mentioned.

00:25:06.816 --> 00:25:10.380
I think it's context specific
too, like which one,

00:25:10.381 --> 00:25:11.580
which metric to use for

00:25:11.581 --> 00:25:13.725
what purpose and so

00:25:13.726 --> 00:25:15.945
when we're talking
about auditing systems,

00:25:15.946 --> 00:25:18.470
the way I think about
it is how to just

00:25:18.471 --> 00:25:21.835
audit it for a whole bunch
of different metrics

00:25:21.836 --> 00:25:24.970
that people have come
up with and then

00:25:24.971 --> 00:25:28.990
maybe when you know the context,

00:25:28.991 --> 00:25:31.165
sometimes maybe having
false positives is

00:25:31.166 --> 00:25:34.060
worse than something else.

00:25:34.061 --> 00:25:37.810
So, maybe then the person who's
using the system can make

00:25:37.811 --> 00:25:42.220
a decision about if this system
is appropriate or not.

00:25:42.221 --> 00:25:43.840
What do you think about laden?

00:25:43.841 --> 00:25:46.440
What is your opinion on
this type of approach?

00:25:46.441 --> 00:25:47.895
Do you think we
should have just one

00:25:47.896 --> 00:25:49.420
or two metrics that we all agree

00:25:49.421 --> 00:25:52.565
on and so from there or?

00:25:52.566 --> 00:25:53.840
&gt;&gt; I agree with you that

00:25:53.841 --> 00:25:56.740
different contexts call
for different things.

00:25:56.741 --> 00:25:59.480
That if I'm trying to

00:25:59.481 --> 00:26:04.130
decide whether to
put someone in jail.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:09.910
The standard for being
certain that that person,

00:26:09.911 --> 00:26:12.980
or keeping someone in
jail really is guilty,

00:26:12.981 --> 00:26:14.460
is very different
than if I'm trying to

00:26:14.461 --> 00:26:16.700
decide whether to
give someone a job.

00:26:16.701 --> 00:26:18.920
An issue that we saw addressed in

00:26:18.921 --> 00:26:21.425
the Kavanaugh hearing
quite recently.

00:26:21.426 --> 00:26:23.810
The standard for putting
someone on the Supreme Court

00:26:23.811 --> 00:26:25.480
versus putting someone behind

00:26:25.481 --> 00:26:27.235
bars should be, I
think, different.

00:26:27.236 --> 00:26:29.660
So, the cost of the false
positives versus false

00:26:29.661 --> 00:26:32.330
negative about that
surely are different.

00:26:32.331 --> 00:26:36.190
So, I agree with

00:26:36.191 --> 00:26:42.430
the idea that context makes
different things salient.

00:26:42.431 --> 00:26:45.140
The cost of false positives
versus false negatives in

00:26:45.141 --> 00:26:47.160
different contexts make
a huge difference.

00:26:47.161 --> 00:26:48.540
For sure, I agree with that.

00:26:48.541 --> 00:26:51.320
But the idea that

00:26:51.321 --> 00:26:55.320
we'll throw all our fairness
measures out there.

00:26:55.321 --> 00:26:57.885
If it passes all of them,

00:26:57.886 --> 00:26:59.360
it's great and if it

00:26:59.361 --> 00:27:01.755
fails all of them,
it's probably bad.

00:27:01.756 --> 00:27:05.680
If it passes some

00:27:05.681 --> 00:27:07.540
and fails some, it's
somewhere in between.

00:27:07.541 --> 00:27:10.585
That's appealing. We were
talking about this last night.

00:27:10.586 --> 00:27:14.590
But I have a worry that
all the interesting cases,

00:27:14.591 --> 00:27:16.150
the ones that really
puzzle us are going

00:27:16.151 --> 00:27:17.890
to be in those in-between cases.

00:27:17.891 --> 00:27:19.790
So, maybe it's not going to be

00:27:19.791 --> 00:27:23.090
that helpful, and that really,

00:27:23.091 --> 00:27:26.095
I think the issues are posed

00:27:26.096 --> 00:27:31.880
because given injustice
in society over time,

00:27:31.881 --> 00:27:34.940
race, sex, and other
protected characteristics

00:27:34.941 --> 00:27:36.640
are going to track with

00:27:36.641 --> 00:27:38.960
a lot of qualities or traits

00:27:38.961 --> 00:27:41.795
that people legitimately
care about.

00:27:41.796 --> 00:27:45.850
So, there's a deep
moral issue about how

00:27:45.851 --> 00:27:50.700
disruptive you want to be
of those correlations.

00:27:50.701 --> 00:27:53.430
That's a moral question or

00:27:53.431 --> 00:27:56.790
a normative question
that we have to address.

00:27:56.791 --> 00:28:00.255
&gt;&gt; Nicole, do you
have a question?

00:28:00.256 --> 00:28:03.800
&gt;&gt; Hi, guys. Thanks.
Very interesting commentary.

00:28:03.801 --> 00:28:06.450
I was curious, a lot

00:28:06.451 --> 00:28:09.350
of the discussion of
fairness so far has been

00:28:09.351 --> 00:28:12.565
about the fairness of
the outcome and I'm wondering if

00:28:12.566 --> 00:28:14.420
there's two different processes

00:28:14.421 --> 00:28:16.345
that lead to that same outcome.

00:28:16.346 --> 00:28:19.250
Is there distinguishing one

00:28:19.251 --> 00:28:20.620
is fair and one is not and we

00:28:20.621 --> 00:28:22.290
should prefer one
process to another,

00:28:22.291 --> 00:28:26.580
or is it just about the outcome?

00:28:26.581 --> 00:28:29.355
&gt;&gt; I think it depends.

00:28:29.356 --> 00:28:31.895
I have trouble keeping it in

00:28:31.896 --> 00:28:34.070
the abstract for some of these

00:28:34.071 --> 00:28:36.940
because the context in
the scenario is so important,

00:28:36.941 --> 00:28:40.025
so tied to how you
would even measure it.

00:28:40.026 --> 00:28:42.140
So, I guess the answer is it

00:28:42.141 --> 00:28:45.025
depends on what the scenario is.

00:28:45.026 --> 00:28:47.755
I don't know, maybe we can
run through some cases-

00:28:47.756 --> 00:28:50.040
&gt;&gt; I think that those are

00:28:50.041 --> 00:28:52.510
different- that process
often matters to fairness.

00:28:52.511 --> 00:28:53.770
In fact, it shows us

00:28:53.771 --> 00:28:55.450
why sometimes we
don't care about say,

00:28:55.451 --> 00:28:57.600
treating light cases
alike in a certain sense.

00:28:57.601 --> 00:28:59.700
That is the
quintessential example

00:28:59.701 --> 00:29:01.005
of a fair procedure is,

00:29:01.006 --> 00:29:03.165
let's flip the coin,
let's draw straws.

00:29:03.166 --> 00:29:05.620
Is a lottery-type system.

00:29:05.621 --> 00:29:09.250
That's not actually trying
to track anything about,

00:29:09.251 --> 00:29:10.630
"I'm really choosing the people

00:29:10.631 --> 00:29:11.800
who have the skills for the job."

00:29:11.801 --> 00:29:15.145
It's just modeling
fairness as procedure.

00:29:15.146 --> 00:29:18.935
So, sometimes we care
about fair procedures.

00:29:18.936 --> 00:29:20.420
Sometimes we care about

00:29:20.421 --> 00:29:22.380
getting the correct results
where those are

00:29:22.381 --> 00:29:24.800
independently determined
and sometimes

00:29:24.801 --> 00:29:27.145
we care about a little bit
of each, I think.

00:29:27.146 --> 00:29:31.240
&gt;&gt; So Mira, I'm interested
in how your team

00:29:31.241 --> 00:29:32.980
actually measures fairness and

00:29:32.981 --> 00:29:35.765
actually tests the systems
that you're building.

00:29:35.766 --> 00:29:38.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I'll
give an example and

00:29:38.771 --> 00:29:42.070
I'll make it- I won't
give the exact example.

00:29:42.071 --> 00:29:44.500
But let's say we were
looking at a system that

00:29:44.501 --> 00:29:45.690
decides who should come into

00:29:45.691 --> 00:29:47.610
this room and who should not.

00:29:47.611 --> 00:29:49.600
One of the things that
we do is we work with

00:29:49.601 --> 00:29:52.645
the team who's making
this design, that algorithm.

00:29:52.646 --> 00:29:54.940
Before we even start
collecting any data,

00:29:54.941 --> 00:29:57.090
we started running through
the scenarios for how we

00:29:57.091 --> 00:29:59.600
might operate around this system.

00:29:59.601 --> 00:30:01.570
So, we identify different
groups of people.

00:30:01.571 --> 00:30:03.770
We call that subgroup
classification.

00:30:03.771 --> 00:30:06.120
So, we're identifying
these different categories

00:30:06.121 --> 00:30:07.625
of people that might
end up using it.

00:30:07.626 --> 00:30:09.570
You might have children
coming in and out on

00:30:09.571 --> 00:30:11.560
the weekend with their families.

00:30:11.561 --> 00:30:14.020
You might have people
with head coverings.

00:30:14.021 --> 00:30:15.870
You might have
people coming in on

00:30:15.871 --> 00:30:19.060
a rainy day and how it changes
the environmental factors.

00:30:19.061 --> 00:30:20.780
So, we start going through all of

00:30:20.781 --> 00:30:23.625
the scenarios and we create
a set of stakeholders.

00:30:23.626 --> 00:30:25.160
We create a set of scenarios.

00:30:25.161 --> 00:30:26.370
Then, we use that to

00:30:26.371 --> 00:30:28.435
decide how we're going
to collect data.

00:30:28.436 --> 00:30:31.440
So, we use that to inform
our data collection effort.

00:30:31.441 --> 00:30:33.615
At the same time that
we're collecting data,

00:30:33.616 --> 00:30:34.740
we start citing, "Hey,

00:30:34.741 --> 00:30:36.630
how do we set benchmarks for

00:30:36.631 --> 00:30:39.010
the outcomes of these subgroups?

00:30:39.011 --> 00:30:41.270
What kind of false positives
do we want to have,

00:30:41.271 --> 00:30:42.900
what kind of error rates
do we want to have?"

00:30:42.901 --> 00:30:45.320
We start coming up with
a model around that.

00:30:45.321 --> 00:30:47.660
Then, as we start
designing our algorithms,

00:30:47.661 --> 00:30:49.380
one of the things
we're trying to do is,

00:30:49.381 --> 00:30:52.255
we've got a whole
platform we use,

00:30:52.256 --> 00:30:54.010
and we call it our
Dev Inner-loop.

00:30:54.011 --> 00:30:55.440
So, it's a place that developers

00:30:55.441 --> 00:30:57.270
go and put their
models in and they

00:30:57.271 --> 00:30:58.940
test it against
test data that they

00:30:58.941 --> 00:31:00.655
might have just collected
with themselves,

00:31:00.656 --> 00:31:02.595
they might have- they
might test it against

00:31:02.596 --> 00:31:06.020
ground truth data that
we've collected explicitly.

00:31:06.021 --> 00:31:10.574
Then, trying to put benchmarks
in place in that platform,

00:31:10.575 --> 00:31:12.410
that can take
our estimates around

00:31:12.411 --> 00:31:14.610
error and false positives
and say, "Okay.

00:31:14.611 --> 00:31:15.980
How are we performing now against

00:31:15.981 --> 00:31:18.270
this very tagged dataset that we

00:31:18.271 --> 00:31:21.115
used during our scenario
identification?"

00:31:21.116 --> 00:31:24.375
So, it's something
we're testing out.

00:31:24.376 --> 00:31:27.445
We haven't finished going
through that entire system yet.

00:31:27.446 --> 00:31:29.840
But I think that
this is something

00:31:29.841 --> 00:31:32.980
that the team hasn't
thought of before.

00:31:32.981 --> 00:31:35.100
I'll give you an example,
we had a piece of

00:31:35.101 --> 00:31:36.990
hardware technology that we were

00:31:36.991 --> 00:31:41.075
using to detect irises
and gaze tracking.

00:31:41.076 --> 00:31:43.600
After a while, we realized
that there was a category of

00:31:43.601 --> 00:31:45.000
people that it just didn't work

00:31:45.001 --> 00:31:46.775
for and we weren't
really sure why.

00:31:46.776 --> 00:31:48.290
We thought we had collected

00:31:48.291 --> 00:31:51.240
tens of thousands of eyeballs.
We thought we had enough.

00:31:51.241 --> 00:31:52.820
Quantity was more important at

00:31:52.821 --> 00:31:55.080
the time instead of quality.

00:31:55.081 --> 00:31:57.030
We realized that green eyeballs

00:31:57.031 --> 00:31:58.935
just perform differently
in the system.

00:31:58.936 --> 00:32:00.450
We didn't have enough of them and

00:32:00.451 --> 00:32:02.315
they were actually hard
for us to collect.

00:32:02.316 --> 00:32:05.720
So, some of the things we
discover after the fact.

00:32:05.721 --> 00:32:08.560
We discover them through
anomalies or bugs.

00:32:08.561 --> 00:32:10.730
Then, it requires us to be really

00:32:10.731 --> 00:32:12.755
thoughtful about
understanding why

00:32:12.756 --> 00:32:14.540
that happened because we may not

00:32:14.541 --> 00:32:16.800
even- it's easy to think
about race and gender,

00:32:16.801 --> 00:32:18.795
and those explicit qualities.

00:32:18.796 --> 00:32:19.910
But then, there are
things that you

00:32:19.911 --> 00:32:21.090
don't typically think about.

00:32:21.091 --> 00:32:23.010
I wouldn't expect
blue and green eyeballs

00:32:23.011 --> 00:32:24.210
to perform differently as we're

00:32:24.211 --> 00:32:26.550
tracking where they're looking.

00:32:26.551 --> 00:32:28.150
That was done a while ago.

00:32:28.151 --> 00:32:29.570
But you can imagine looking

00:32:29.571 --> 00:32:31.000
at the system and
how it's performing.

00:32:31.001 --> 00:32:33.459
Where you see bugs and anomalies,

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:35.690
we have to go and dig
deeper into that and do

00:32:35.691 --> 00:32:38.290
a little bit more work on it.

00:32:38.291 --> 00:32:41.795
&gt;&gt; Great. So, you
mentioned in your answer

00:32:41.796 --> 00:32:45.255
a couple of different
application areas there.

00:32:45.256 --> 00:32:48.920
So, seeing who should be
let into a room or not,

00:32:48.921 --> 00:32:52.265
maybe tracking somebody's eyes
for some purpose.

00:32:52.266 --> 00:32:53.970
Then, there's a bunch
of other areas

00:32:53.971 --> 00:32:55.090
that you can think of where

00:32:55.091 --> 00:32:56.230
Machine Learning is either

00:32:56.231 --> 00:32:58.430
currently used or could be used.

00:32:58.431 --> 00:33:00.330
I'm interested in getting

00:33:00.331 --> 00:33:04.020
some perspectives from
the panelists about what you

00:33:04.021 --> 00:33:06.490
think are the most
important areas in which

00:33:06.491 --> 00:33:10.550
tech companies will
face fairness issues.

00:33:10.800 --> 00:33:13.660
&gt;&gt; I can start with a couple.

00:33:13.661 --> 00:33:16.595
One is, sorry.

00:33:16.596 --> 00:33:21.810
One is, there's a whole-
employment is one example.

00:33:21.811 --> 00:33:25.400
So, there was a whole panel
about LinkedIn before.

00:33:25.401 --> 00:33:28.590
So, LinkedIn is a platform where

00:33:28.591 --> 00:33:31.660
it directly gives you
access to jobs, let's say.

00:33:31.661 --> 00:33:34.060
But there's roundabout
ways in which you

00:33:34.061 --> 00:33:36.570
can also face these issues.

00:33:36.571 --> 00:33:38.680
So, for example, there's
a company called

00:33:38.681 --> 00:33:41.480
HireVue that records
people on camera.

00:33:41.481 --> 00:33:43.925
Then, does a motion
recognition on them.

00:33:43.926 --> 00:33:46.790
Then, gives that information
to the people who

00:33:46.791 --> 00:33:51.380
are interviewing,
the interviewers.

00:33:51.381 --> 00:33:54.200
So, the people who are providing,

00:33:54.201 --> 00:33:55.485
like Affectiva is providing

00:33:55.486 --> 00:33:58.665
emotion recognition technology
to this company.

00:33:58.666 --> 00:34:00.510
I don't know how that would

00:34:00.511 --> 00:34:05.490
work if it was found that
they were discriminating-

00:34:05.491 --> 00:34:07.420
By certain attributes,

00:34:07.421 --> 00:34:09.790
and then that was due
to that technology,

00:34:09.791 --> 00:34:11.590
the emotion
recognition technology

00:34:11.591 --> 00:34:12.760
or other things that
they were using

00:34:12.761 --> 00:34:17.320
from this third-party company.

00:34:17.321 --> 00:34:19.540
I think you can
face these kinds of

00:34:19.541 --> 00:34:21.610
things in a roundabout
manner too,

00:34:21.611 --> 00:34:23.005
even if you're providing

00:34:23.006 --> 00:34:24.220
face recognition technology or

00:34:24.221 --> 00:34:26.350
some other thing
that then is used by

00:34:26.351 --> 00:34:31.360
some third-party entity to

00:34:31.361 --> 00:34:34.160
make decisions about
people's lives.

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:38.440
&gt;&gt; I would agree that hiring
is a great example of

00:34:38.441 --> 00:34:42.180
a domain where you want to
be fair, especially because,

00:34:42.181 --> 00:34:43.590
for what Tim had said before,

00:34:43.591 --> 00:34:46.040
that if you have
a diverse group of people,

00:34:46.041 --> 00:34:49.455
you can make sure your
products are fairer as well.

00:34:49.456 --> 00:34:51.940
So, there's kind of
a compounding effect there.

00:34:51.941 --> 00:34:53.380
If your products
are not working in

00:34:53.381 --> 00:34:55.460
certain neighborhoods or for
certain groups of people,

00:34:55.461 --> 00:34:57.015
you're more likely to notice that

00:34:57.016 --> 00:34:58.810
if you have a diverse workforce.

00:34:58.811 --> 00:35:00.100
So, they can figure

00:35:00.101 --> 00:35:02.170
out hiring algorithms
with the help of that.

00:35:02.171 --> 00:35:03.945
&gt;&gt; We have a question over here.

00:35:03.946 --> 00:35:05.990
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I do. I
guess in large part,

00:35:05.991 --> 00:35:07.465
it's probably for Deborah,

00:35:07.466 --> 00:35:08.985
but I'm not sure.

00:35:08.986 --> 00:35:11.150
So, one thing that
came out, especially,

00:35:11.151 --> 00:35:13.200
say obligations like hiring,

00:35:13.201 --> 00:35:14.390
and actually from your

00:35:14.391 --> 00:35:16.370
story when I started
raising my hand,

00:35:16.371 --> 00:35:18.180
that one way to figure out

00:35:18.181 --> 00:35:21.155
fairness is to test
for these groups.

00:35:21.156 --> 00:35:25.300
Test if the face
recognition works just

00:35:25.301 --> 00:35:27.490
as well for very dark faces

00:35:27.491 --> 00:35:29.715
than for light faces
or some other.

00:35:29.716 --> 00:35:33.410
But there is aspiration
of fairness.

00:35:33.411 --> 00:35:36.110
They're protected, legally
protected attributes

00:35:36.111 --> 00:35:39.070
that we may not
discriminate based on sex,

00:35:39.071 --> 00:35:41.320
and race, and whatnot.

00:35:41.321 --> 00:35:45.465
I guess this is
data-driven world.

00:35:45.466 --> 00:35:47.340
I think it's a very natural idea

00:35:47.341 --> 00:35:49.290
that the only way we can refer.

00:35:49.291 --> 00:35:50.865
If we actually use

00:35:50.866 --> 00:35:53.390
the protective attribute in

00:35:53.391 --> 00:35:56.090
our decision-making for
the obvious reason,

00:35:56.091 --> 00:35:58.035
because the protected
attribute is so

00:35:58.036 --> 00:36:00.200
correlated with a lot
of other stuff,

00:36:00.201 --> 00:36:02.460
that not using it doesn't

00:36:02.461 --> 00:36:05.545
allow us to even test for
things you're testing for.

00:36:05.546 --> 00:36:08.430
But that raises a legal question
and maybe all of you,

00:36:08.431 --> 00:36:11.525
but you're the lawyers,
so that's maybe you.

00:36:11.526 --> 00:36:14.845
Will the legal community
accept this?

00:36:14.846 --> 00:36:18.840
That in order to be fair
to the protected groups,

00:36:18.841 --> 00:36:22.020
the systems actually have to
use the protected attribute,

00:36:22.021 --> 00:36:23.360
that's the way to make it fair,

00:36:23.361 --> 00:36:26.530
rather than the idea that

00:36:26.531 --> 00:36:30.395
we are fair to women if we
don't use sex as an attribute.

00:36:30.396 --> 00:36:33.565
&gt;&gt; Right. So, the legal
system unfortunately,

00:36:33.566 --> 00:36:37.210
perhaps has glams onto

00:36:37.211 --> 00:36:39.620
our focuses onto
whether you use race,

00:36:39.621 --> 00:36:42.120
sex, or some other
protected classifications

00:36:42.121 --> 00:36:43.885
as a classification.

00:36:43.886 --> 00:36:45.170
But you have to separate

00:36:45.171 --> 00:36:46.990
two different ways
that you might use it,

00:36:46.991 --> 00:36:49.310
so that I'm extrapolating,

00:36:49.311 --> 00:36:50.610
I think from what
you said, you tell

00:36:50.611 --> 00:36:52.085
me if I had gotten it right.

00:36:52.086 --> 00:36:55.090
So, suppose you have an algorithm

00:36:55.091 --> 00:36:58.000
that doesn't use race or sex,

00:36:58.001 --> 00:37:00.340
but after it pops out
a bunch of things,

00:37:00.341 --> 00:37:02.130
you check to see

00:37:02.131 --> 00:37:05.980
whether there are
very few women in the pool,

00:37:05.981 --> 00:37:08.160
or very few racial
minorities in the pool.

00:37:08.161 --> 00:37:10.625
So, you're looking
at the results in

00:37:10.626 --> 00:37:13.635
terms of the racial
classifications produced,

00:37:13.636 --> 00:37:15.190
and then thinking, well,

00:37:15.191 --> 00:37:18.700
that's too much of

00:37:18.701 --> 00:37:21.430
a disparate impact on women
or racial minorities,

00:37:21.431 --> 00:37:26.675
so let's revisit the stuff
that goes into the algorithm.

00:37:26.676 --> 00:37:29.780
That I think it's
perfectly permissible.

00:37:29.781 --> 00:37:31.170
I think in the literature,

00:37:31.171 --> 00:37:32.430
there's been this worry that

00:37:32.431 --> 00:37:34.745
the court's decision
and reaching somehow,

00:37:34.746 --> 00:37:37.020
which I can talk about
later or whatever,

00:37:37.021 --> 00:37:38.260
somehow doesn't allow that.

00:37:38.261 --> 00:37:40.450
I think that's
a dramatic overreading,

00:37:40.451 --> 00:37:42.710
which I think that's
perfectly permissible.

00:37:42.711 --> 00:37:44.890
But if one of the traits
that's actually

00:37:44.891 --> 00:37:47.255
in your algorithm is race or sex,

00:37:47.256 --> 00:37:49.250
I think you will get
into legal trouble,

00:37:49.251 --> 00:37:53.570
and I think what you point
out about that is that's

00:37:53.571 --> 00:37:59.070
an unfortunate fact
of the way that

00:37:59.071 --> 00:38:06.105
currently our legal system
understands what's prohibited.

00:38:06.106 --> 00:38:09.970
It's not something that's
easy to fix because it's

00:38:09.971 --> 00:38:13.680
the court's interpretation
of constitutional law.

00:38:13.681 --> 00:38:15.535
So, let me say that it's

00:38:15.536 --> 00:38:17.860
somewhat easy to fix if you're
talking about employers,

00:38:17.861 --> 00:38:19.330
but not if you're talking
about the government.

00:38:19.331 --> 00:38:21.985
So, the constitution only
affects governmental actors,

00:38:21.986 --> 00:38:24.965
but there are a lot of
governmental actors.

00:38:24.966 --> 00:38:26.680
&gt;&gt; I just want to jump in here.

00:38:26.681 --> 00:38:28.240
I recognize that
I'm not a panelist,

00:38:28.241 --> 00:38:29.715
so I will keep this very brief.

00:38:29.716 --> 00:38:32.360
I did write a paper on
exactly this topic,

00:38:32.361 --> 00:38:33.840
this summer with a lawyer,

00:38:33.841 --> 00:38:35.940
where we looked at

00:38:35.941 --> 00:38:39.015
really the lower and specifically
disparate treatment,

00:38:39.016 --> 00:38:41.365
and thought about ways
in which it might

00:38:41.366 --> 00:38:43.680
be okay to use those attributes.

00:38:43.681 --> 00:38:45.450
One of the scenarios
that we looked

00:38:45.451 --> 00:38:47.315
at was the case
where those kinds of

00:38:47.316 --> 00:38:50.640
individual level sensitive
attributes are used during

00:38:50.641 --> 00:38:54.220
the training process to
develop your classifier,

00:38:54.221 --> 00:38:56.075
but then at deployment time,

00:38:56.076 --> 00:38:58.840
it has no such access
to those attributes.

00:38:58.841 --> 00:39:00.510
We argue that that is fine

00:39:00.511 --> 00:39:02.710
from a disparate
treatment perspective.

00:39:02.711 --> 00:39:05.050
The paper is not
currently online,

00:39:05.051 --> 00:39:07.450
but probably will be online
within the next few weeks.

00:39:07.451 --> 00:39:08.480
I will email you,

00:39:08.481 --> 00:39:09.760
and let you know when it is.

00:39:09.761 --> 00:39:12.030
&gt;&gt; Can I ask a very
quick question? Sorry.

00:39:12.031 --> 00:39:15.330
&gt;&gt; Thank you. I guess it
just is a follow-up comment.

00:39:15.331 --> 00:39:17.650
I thought that this
would during the break.

00:39:17.651 --> 00:39:24.965
Before the discrimination cases
for college admission,

00:39:24.966 --> 00:39:28.165
Cornell Engineering used to have

00:39:28.166 --> 00:39:30.500
weighing differently
the SAT scores

00:39:30.501 --> 00:39:32.185
for women than for men,

00:39:32.186 --> 00:39:33.600
and the argument they made,

00:39:33.601 --> 00:39:36.025
which I actually
support as an argument,

00:39:36.026 --> 00:39:37.980
that SAT course on its own and

00:39:37.981 --> 00:39:40.495
not something we care about.

00:39:40.496 --> 00:39:44.450
We care about them as a predictor
of college performance,

00:39:44.451 --> 00:39:46.310
and they had data to show

00:39:46.311 --> 00:39:50.190
that the lower SAT
course for women,

00:39:50.191 --> 00:39:53.565
same as SAT scores for
women predicts higher GPA.

00:39:53.566 --> 00:39:57.110
Therefore, they can weigh
up the GPAs for women.

00:39:57.111 --> 00:39:59.895
They no longer do it because
I guess it's not legal,

00:39:59.896 --> 00:40:02.810
but it's kind of said,
they had a point.

00:40:02.811 --> 00:40:06.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I have
a much more general question,

00:40:06.661 --> 00:40:10.640
which is that we are
very focused on US law,

00:40:10.641 --> 00:40:13.385
and we have companies that
are operating globally.

00:40:13.386 --> 00:40:15.210
We have Machine
Learning conferences

00:40:15.211 --> 00:40:16.585
that are international,

00:40:16.586 --> 00:40:18.380
researchers that
are international,

00:40:18.381 --> 00:40:20.555
and this area of fairness is

00:40:20.556 --> 00:40:24.560
intertwined with legal systems.

00:40:24.561 --> 00:40:27.570
I mean, I don't know.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:31.480
Our algorithms that
were coming up with,

00:40:31.481 --> 00:40:33.040
the scenarios we're
coming up with are

00:40:33.041 --> 00:40:35.155
very intertwined with US law,

00:40:35.156 --> 00:40:39.535
and I don't know how to
address that. What do we do?

00:40:39.536 --> 00:40:42.720
Do you have different
researchers in different places,

00:40:42.721 --> 00:40:46.340
and addressing it based
on different legalities.

00:40:46.341 --> 00:40:48.720
Should we have
one more international law

00:40:48.721 --> 00:40:50.085
or something like this because-

00:40:50.086 --> 00:40:51.960
&gt;&gt; This comes up in
privacy as well,

00:40:51.961 --> 00:40:53.950
especially with the GDPR
at the movement,

00:40:53.951 --> 00:40:55.625
which is specifically Europe.

00:40:55.626 --> 00:40:57.850
But yet, we look at
companies like Microsoft,

00:40:57.851 --> 00:40:59.625
that are international companies,

00:40:59.626 --> 00:41:02.410
and suddenly, that is
applied everywhere,

00:41:02.411 --> 00:41:03.940
not just within Europe.

00:41:03.941 --> 00:41:06.050
So, I think there's
perhaps stuff we can

00:41:06.051 --> 00:41:08.190
learn from some of
these other disciplines as well.

00:41:08.191 --> 00:41:09.300
&gt;&gt; I think it also ties into

00:41:09.301 --> 00:41:10.970
the first panel on
long-term thinking,

00:41:10.971 --> 00:41:13.930
where maybe we should think
about what is ethical or

00:41:13.931 --> 00:41:18.295
fair in a philosophical way
also as researchers,

00:41:18.296 --> 00:41:20.480
and then maybe overtime laws

00:41:20.481 --> 00:41:22.135
will change or
catch up with that.

00:41:22.136 --> 00:41:25.640
&gt;&gt; That's also
a very Western-centric view,

00:41:25.641 --> 00:41:27.920
and one of the things we debate,

00:41:27.921 --> 00:41:29.340
ad nauseam in our group,

00:41:29.341 --> 00:41:31.280
is are we pushing too much of

00:41:31.281 --> 00:41:33.620
a Western-centric
viewpoints because

00:41:33.621 --> 00:41:36.165
we are an international
corporation.

00:41:36.166 --> 00:41:38.515
When you talk about privacy,

00:41:38.516 --> 00:41:40.140
and you look at other cultures,

00:41:40.141 --> 00:41:43.410
large parts of
the world view privacy

00:41:43.411 --> 00:41:45.110
and fairness quite
differently than you

00:41:45.111 --> 00:41:47.550
do in the Western side
of the world.

00:41:47.551 --> 00:41:49.780
It's not something we have
resolved anything yet,

00:41:49.781 --> 00:41:52.610
but it is a constant source
of tension and debate

00:41:52.611 --> 00:41:58.160
because there are
such different viewpoints on it.

00:41:58.161 --> 00:42:00.470
Certain cultures don't emphasize

00:42:00.471 --> 00:42:02.960
individuals in the same way
versus collective,

00:42:02.961 --> 00:42:04.720
and so trying to

00:42:04.721 --> 00:42:08.090
navigate that is
a massive challenge for us.

00:42:08.091 --> 00:42:09.715
&gt;&gt; Question over there?

00:42:09.716 --> 00:42:13.785
&gt;&gt; There's a kind of
[inaudible] reinforce that.

00:42:13.786 --> 00:42:16.210
Sometimes show
things that occur in

00:42:16.211 --> 00:42:18.700
one jurisdiction
become best practice,

00:42:18.701 --> 00:42:21.340
and a lot of a number of
jurisdictions adopt them,

00:42:21.341 --> 00:42:24.025
build on that side of
the GDPR, Denmark.

00:42:24.026 --> 00:42:27.700
They came out of Europe,
America [inaudible] today.

00:42:27.701 --> 00:42:28.990
So, if you have
lived in the West,

00:42:28.991 --> 00:42:31.440
we're [inaudible] There are

00:42:31.441 --> 00:42:34.695
other areas where governments
willing to accept that,

00:42:34.696 --> 00:42:38.380
the rights of US government
to access data,

00:42:38.381 --> 00:42:40.570
all things that make
your main governments in

00:42:40.571 --> 00:42:42.985
particular want data
kept in Europe,

00:42:42.986 --> 00:42:44.980
but like I said, it's
kind of protections.

00:42:44.981 --> 00:42:47.930
So, there is a very old
question of what does

00:42:47.931 --> 00:42:50.190
internationalization mean with

00:42:50.191 --> 00:42:52.620
a whole bunch of these questions,

00:42:52.621 --> 00:42:54.370
and for companies like Microsoft

00:42:54.371 --> 00:42:56.205
who sells products globally,

00:42:56.206 --> 00:42:58.230
how do you parameterize
those things?

00:42:58.231 --> 00:43:00.940
It's not as easy as changing
the language is in and out.

00:43:00.941 --> 00:43:02.710
You're changing
moral codes in and out,

00:43:02.711 --> 00:43:04.080
and how do you
represent that's in

00:43:04.081 --> 00:43:05.645
your systems, your technology?

00:43:05.646 --> 00:43:07.170
&gt;&gt; So, what I was
going to say that

00:43:07.171 --> 00:43:10.770
I think relates to that point,

00:43:10.771 --> 00:43:13.980
is that it's one thing
if one legal regime

00:43:13.981 --> 00:43:17.745
has a standard here and
another one is just higher,

00:43:17.746 --> 00:43:19.440
but it's a totally
different thing

00:43:19.441 --> 00:43:21.255
if they actually conflict.

00:43:21.256 --> 00:43:23.065
I think around discrimination,

00:43:23.066 --> 00:43:25.000
there more potentially
in conflict.

00:43:25.001 --> 00:43:26.950
So, the problems are
a little deeper than

00:43:26.951 --> 00:43:29.685
just a higher floor.

00:43:29.686 --> 00:43:32.145
&gt;&gt; I mean, just to build
on that a little bit.

00:43:32.146 --> 00:43:36.150
So, Brad Smith published
a blog post calling for

00:43:36.151 --> 00:43:37.660
government regulation of

00:43:37.661 --> 00:43:40.680
face recognition
technology in July.

00:43:40.681 --> 00:43:44.130
I want to sort of dig
into this and say,

00:43:44.131 --> 00:43:46.185
"Is that the right approach,

00:43:46.186 --> 00:43:48.715
and who should be
making decisions about

00:43:48.716 --> 00:43:52.100
what makes a system
fair or ethically okay.

00:43:52.101 --> 00:43:53.960
Is it the government?

00:43:53.961 --> 00:43:55.760
Which government?

00:43:55.761 --> 00:43:58.130
How do you reconcile
those differences?

00:43:58.131 --> 00:44:01.815
Or is it companies like
Microsoft, or developers,

00:44:01.816 --> 00:44:03.460
or perhaps the people
who are going

00:44:03.461 --> 00:44:06.180
to be deploying these systems?"

00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:11.265
&gt;&gt; So Deborah and I were talking
about this during lunch,

00:44:11.266 --> 00:44:13.190
I think and in

00:44:13.191 --> 00:44:16.465
some cases you have legal
president to set the floor,

00:44:16.466 --> 00:44:19.125
and we come in and
say, you know what?

00:44:19.126 --> 00:44:21.110
We don't think the floor is
acceptable enough and let's

00:44:21.111 --> 00:44:23.120
go and notch higher
two notches higher.

00:44:23.121 --> 00:44:26.470
Other instances like
facial recognition technologies,

00:44:26.471 --> 00:44:30.160
there is no floor yet set
by the government and so,

00:44:30.161 --> 00:44:31.530
we could go in and
say here's what we

00:44:31.531 --> 00:44:33.595
think of potential Flores.

00:44:33.596 --> 00:44:36.280
And Brad Smith posts what he

00:44:36.281 --> 00:44:38.900
called on was legislation
to to be used in

00:44:38.901 --> 00:44:41.400
this category because it's

00:44:41.401 --> 00:44:43.520
such a critical piece
and not just

00:44:43.521 --> 00:44:46.450
facial recognition but I
also think biometrics.

00:44:46.451 --> 00:44:48.385
So, in that one maybe be

00:44:48.386 --> 00:44:51.030
corporations who are
doing technology work in

00:44:51.031 --> 00:44:52.575
this space work alongside with

00:44:52.576 --> 00:44:54.900
regulators to define the space,

00:44:54.901 --> 00:44:57.340
educate each other, and

00:44:57.341 --> 00:45:02.390
sets a floor around
behavior there.

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:05.530
&gt;&gt; I would agree with
them. Just add one thing

00:45:05.531 --> 00:45:10.140
that legal answers or

00:45:10.141 --> 00:45:13.550
legal interventions are going
to be more cumbersome and

00:45:13.551 --> 00:45:18.510
often after the fact and
laws often tend to be more.

00:45:18.511 --> 00:45:20.940
They're not as targeted to

00:45:20.941 --> 00:45:24.730
a specific problem that occurs.

00:45:24.731 --> 00:45:29.060
So, I think even if you
have the law coming in

00:45:29.061 --> 00:45:30.460
there's always going
to be a room for

00:45:30.461 --> 00:45:33.725
companies to supplement that.

00:45:33.726 --> 00:45:36.970
&gt;&gt; So, I guess I was

00:45:36.971 --> 00:45:38.340
rather hoping an economist

00:45:38.341 --> 00:45:39.605
would ask this question though,

00:45:39.606 --> 00:45:41.915
what's wrong with just
letting the market fix it?

00:45:41.916 --> 00:45:45.130
If your face recognition
software is bias then

00:45:45.131 --> 00:45:46.755
people aren't going to
buy it and if you have

00:45:46.756 --> 00:45:49.500
these accurate data sheets then,

00:45:49.501 --> 00:45:51.780
they know what they're
getting and they can

00:45:51.781 --> 00:45:53.875
just not buy something
that doesn't

00:45:53.876 --> 00:45:55.170
do what they want it to do

00:45:55.171 --> 00:45:57.970
and if they do buy it and
they release a product

00:45:57.971 --> 00:46:00.140
that is bias then people

00:46:00.141 --> 00:46:02.450
aren't going to buy the product
and the company will go-.

00:46:02.451 --> 00:46:05.230
&gt;&gt; I think the issue
is like for me,

00:46:05.231 --> 00:46:09.850
it's pretty crazy that it took
for Joy [inaudible] paper for

00:46:09.851 --> 00:46:11.980
like Kamala Harris deciding when

00:46:11.981 --> 00:46:14.745
she's writing to the FBI
and things like this.

00:46:14.746 --> 00:46:19.240
People have been using
face recognition forever, right?

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:22.855
They've been using
in law enforcement,

00:46:22.856 --> 00:46:24.340
companies have been using it.

00:46:24.341 --> 00:46:26.760
So, even though it was bias

00:46:26.761 --> 00:46:29.420
for a long time people
have been using it.

00:46:29.421 --> 00:46:31.195
So for me I'm not an economist,

00:46:31.196 --> 00:46:33.600
so, I don't know
much about markets.

00:46:33.601 --> 00:46:38.705
But for me, this says the market
hasn't been fixing it.

00:46:38.706 --> 00:46:41.480
Also, a lot of times I think that

00:46:41.481 --> 00:46:42.780
when you have something that

00:46:42.781 --> 00:46:44.800
unfairly punishes minorities,

00:46:44.801 --> 00:46:46.130
it could be a minority of

00:46:46.131 --> 00:46:48.010
people who don't
really have a voice,

00:46:48.011 --> 00:46:51.250
I don't know if the market
helps these people, right?

00:46:51.251 --> 00:46:53.680
I think this is
legislature in my opinion.

00:46:53.681 --> 00:46:55.040
&gt;&gt; So, I mean, the point of

00:46:55.041 --> 00:46:57.720
anti-discrimination law
is to protect groups that

00:46:57.721 --> 00:46:59.550
don't have either the
political power or

00:46:59.551 --> 00:47:00.590
the economic power to

00:47:00.591 --> 00:47:02.260
protect themselves
in the marketplace,

00:47:02.261 --> 00:47:04.000
which is why I'm
actually not worried

00:47:04.001 --> 00:47:06.725
about the green
eyed eyeball thing

00:47:06.726 --> 00:47:09.560
because I think actually

00:47:09.561 --> 00:47:12.190
you would expect the market
to self-correct.

00:47:12.191 --> 00:47:14.350
That the point of
anti-discrimination law

00:47:14.351 --> 00:47:16.280
is to protect those groups that

00:47:16.281 --> 00:47:18.070
historically haven't been able

00:47:18.071 --> 00:47:21.920
either economically or politically
to protect themselves.

00:47:21.930 --> 00:47:23.830
&gt;&gt; Question over there.

00:47:23.831 --> 00:47:26.005
&gt;&gt; I keep on saying
minority and then Joy

00:47:26.006 --> 00:47:28.390
always said that under
sampled majority,

00:47:28.391 --> 00:47:29.950
I love that term, so
I'm going to correct

00:47:29.951 --> 00:47:32.240
myself, under sample majority.

00:47:32.241 --> 00:47:34.750
&gt;&gt; Which is a good
example of my point,

00:47:34.751 --> 00:47:37.580
the data sheets
are a good step in

00:47:37.581 --> 00:47:39.530
the right direction
but if somebody who

00:47:39.531 --> 00:47:41.945
studies metadata and
information retrieval,

00:47:41.946 --> 00:47:44.480
metadata itself is socially

00:47:44.481 --> 00:47:46.920
constructed and has lots
of biases built-in.

00:47:46.921 --> 00:47:51.515
So, what we really need here
is a governance process.

00:47:51.516 --> 00:47:53.655
Who's going to watch the watchers

00:47:53.656 --> 00:47:55.780
around metadata processes too.

00:47:55.781 --> 00:47:58.830
So I'm going to add that
to fairness issues.

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:02.910
&gt;&gt; Great. Another
question over there.

00:48:02.911 --> 00:48:05.050
&gt;&gt; It was actually
more of a comment,

00:48:05.051 --> 00:48:06.795
I was mentioning to you before.

00:48:06.796 --> 00:48:07.980
I'm Tina [inaudible] ,

00:48:07.981 --> 00:48:09.120
for those of you who
don't know me I'm

00:48:09.121 --> 00:48:11.140
a professor at
Northeastern University.

00:48:11.141 --> 00:48:14.795
I think is not the job of
Machine Learning to fix

00:48:14.796 --> 00:48:16.670
the biases that are
in the data because

00:48:16.671 --> 00:48:18.630
our society is broken.

00:48:18.631 --> 00:48:22.100
I think the job of
Machine Learning is to show that

00:48:22.101 --> 00:48:23.820
the policies are
not being enforced

00:48:23.821 --> 00:48:26.905
correctly and go back and
change those policies.

00:48:26.906 --> 00:48:28.670
So as opposed to using
Machine Learning

00:48:28.671 --> 00:48:29.940
to make human decisions,

00:48:29.941 --> 00:48:31.300
we should use Machine Learning to

00:48:31.301 --> 00:48:32.900
show that the data that's

00:48:32.901 --> 00:48:35.940
coming out of the enforcements
of the policies are bad,

00:48:35.941 --> 00:48:37.720
go to policymakers and have

00:48:37.721 --> 00:48:39.260
the policies get changed

00:48:39.261 --> 00:48:41.920
or the enforcement
of it get changed.

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:46.330
&gt;&gt; How would that work in
like let's say hiring?

00:48:46.331 --> 00:48:49.900
So, I'm trying to
understand your comment.

00:48:49.901 --> 00:48:53.590
So, when you have
an automated tool that is

00:48:53.591 --> 00:48:57.575
then shown to break certain laws
that already exist like,

00:48:57.576 --> 00:48:58.820
let's say Equal Employment

00:48:58.821 --> 00:49:00.605
Opportunity Act or
something like that.

00:49:00.606 --> 00:49:04.670
Then, don't you think
that the automated tool

00:49:04.671 --> 00:49:08.950
itself should change
to not break that law?

00:49:08.951 --> 00:49:10.980
&gt;&gt; So the question is that is

00:49:10.981 --> 00:49:13.505
the algorithm doing
the wrong thing

00:49:13.506 --> 00:49:15.570
or is the data that you're

00:49:15.571 --> 00:49:18.140
getting from the enforcement
of the policy,

00:49:18.141 --> 00:49:19.945
let's say stop-and-frisk, right?

00:49:19.946 --> 00:49:22.210
So, there's a stop
and frisk policy,

00:49:22.211 --> 00:49:25.245
I have cameras on cops,
it's taking data,

00:49:25.246 --> 00:49:27.540
and if I can from that

00:49:27.541 --> 00:49:30.160
data reconstruct
the intent of the policy,

00:49:30.161 --> 00:49:31.695
and then say look.

00:49:31.696 --> 00:49:33.905
Did you intend to
your policy to do

00:49:33.906 --> 00:49:36.815
X to the policy maker?

00:49:36.816 --> 00:49:39.305
Right? If the person says yes,

00:49:39.306 --> 00:49:41.330
and that the intent is oh,

00:49:41.331 --> 00:49:43.650
wait a second
stop-and-frisk is stopping

00:49:43.651 --> 00:49:46.755
black and brown people
more than others.

00:49:46.756 --> 00:49:48.385
What is your intent?

00:49:48.386 --> 00:49:50.400
I bet most policymakers
will say no,

00:49:50.401 --> 00:49:52.860
that was not my intent
of stop-and-frisk.

00:49:52.861 --> 00:49:54.700
But the question is
how do you actually

00:49:54.701 --> 00:49:56.810
change society not that, oh,

00:49:56.811 --> 00:49:58.620
I have to come up with
the Machine Learning algorithm

00:49:58.621 --> 00:49:59.630
because it's going to be used

00:49:59.631 --> 00:50:01.000
in human decision-making
because we

00:50:01.001 --> 00:50:02.715
have all this data
that's coming out.

00:50:02.716 --> 00:50:04.580
Because a human
decision makers are using it

00:50:04.581 --> 00:50:06.785
mostly for efficiency
purposes, right?

00:50:06.786 --> 00:50:08.619
That's why they like
risk assessments,

00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:10.900
which are very bad in
my perspective because they

00:50:10.901 --> 00:50:12.150
don't give you any uncertainty

00:50:12.151 --> 00:50:13.165
about the algorithm, right?

00:50:13.166 --> 00:50:14.670
Eight, right?

00:50:14.671 --> 00:50:15.755
So, Tina goes to jail.

00:50:15.756 --> 00:50:17.160
&gt;&gt; I don't think we
would disagree that it's

00:50:17.161 --> 00:50:19.390
a bad idea to use
data to point out

00:50:19.391 --> 00:50:20.460
problems we have with our

00:50:20.461 --> 00:50:22.370
policies but at the same time or

00:50:22.371 --> 00:50:24.500
systems are being used already

00:50:24.501 --> 00:50:27.180
today in the world which
is already biased.

00:50:27.181 --> 00:50:28.540
So you have have to make

00:50:28.541 --> 00:50:31.390
decisions and you can
say as a company,

00:50:31.391 --> 00:50:34.060
well it's unfair
that the number of

00:50:34.061 --> 00:50:36.950
applicants of minorities
and women for

00:50:36.951 --> 00:50:39.535
computer companies is
unbalanced and that poses

00:50:39.536 --> 00:50:40.850
all kinds of problems we have in

00:50:40.851 --> 00:50:42.845
our educational system
and earlier on.

00:50:42.846 --> 00:50:44.890
But we don't want to just
say let's wait until they

00:50:44.891 --> 00:50:47.200
solve that and then
when we have 50,

00:50:47.201 --> 00:50:49.170
50, we have to make
decisions today and

00:50:49.171 --> 00:50:50.505
the algorithms have
to make decisions

00:50:50.506 --> 00:50:52.090
in where we are right now.

00:50:52.091 --> 00:50:54.650
&gt;&gt; So, I guess my answer
to that is that it's

00:50:54.651 --> 00:50:55.940
not that we have to wait is

00:50:55.941 --> 00:50:57.350
that it has to be in parallel.

00:50:57.351 --> 00:50:57.730
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:50:57.731 --> 00:50:59.000
&gt;&gt; Right? The other one is

00:50:59.001 --> 00:51:00.600
that what are the incentives are

00:51:00.601 --> 00:51:01.760
there decision makers that are

00:51:01.761 --> 00:51:03.715
using your algorithms, right?

00:51:03.716 --> 00:51:05.310
Because it wasn't like before,

00:51:05.311 --> 00:51:07.375
they were using the data, right?

00:51:07.376 --> 00:51:10.345
So obviously, we have
stupid algorithms,

00:51:10.346 --> 00:51:12.340
like mandatory minimum
sentencing that

00:51:12.341 --> 00:51:14.270
do not look at data, right?

00:51:14.271 --> 00:51:15.695
But the question is,

00:51:15.696 --> 00:51:17.640
you have to take into
account incentives

00:51:17.641 --> 00:51:19.595
of the people who are
using your algorithms,

00:51:19.596 --> 00:51:21.920
and I don't think people are
really looking into that.

00:51:21.921 --> 00:51:25.140
&gt;&gt; So, I think all the cases

00:51:25.141 --> 00:51:27.670
don't look like the
stop-and-frisk kind of case,

00:51:27.671 --> 00:51:31.460
that there are
some cases where there

00:51:31.461 --> 00:51:35.270
are injustices or
unfairness in the past

00:51:35.271 --> 00:51:37.860
that I think those

00:51:37.861 --> 00:51:39.990
of us who are in
this world carrying about

00:51:39.991 --> 00:51:42.480
algorithms don't want to compound

00:51:42.481 --> 00:51:44.120
those prior injustices and

00:51:44.121 --> 00:51:45.280
that's an independent concerns.

00:51:45.281 --> 00:51:49.685
So one example I like to use
is insurance companies who,

00:51:49.686 --> 00:51:52.440
and this doesn't involve
any complicated AI

00:51:52.441 --> 00:51:55.335
but it would charge
domestic abuse victims.

00:51:55.336 --> 00:51:57.880
&gt;&gt; More for health
and life insurance

00:51:57.881 --> 00:51:59.050
because if you're being beaten up

00:51:59.051 --> 00:52:00.310
by your husband or boyfriend,

00:52:00.311 --> 00:52:02.790
you are more likely to need
health or life insurance.

00:52:02.791 --> 00:52:05.260
Now, the insurance company
wasn't the one that was

00:52:05.261 --> 00:52:08.470
responsible for the women
being beaten up.

00:52:08.471 --> 00:52:10.960
In fact, even after you
left your batterer,

00:52:10.961 --> 00:52:12.370
you were most at risk.

00:52:12.371 --> 00:52:13.625
So, they would charge them

00:52:13.626 --> 00:52:16.250
higher rates even if they've
left their batterer.

00:52:16.251 --> 00:52:19.390
But, I think the response
that most people have is,

00:52:19.391 --> 00:52:21.110
there's something
unfair about that.

00:52:21.111 --> 00:52:24.890
It's not an accurate reflection

00:52:24.891 --> 00:52:27.110
of their actuarial risk and it's

00:52:27.111 --> 00:52:29.850
not that the insurance
company is somehow

00:52:29.851 --> 00:52:31.195
responsible for

00:52:31.196 --> 00:52:33.340
perpetrating the violence
against these women,

00:52:33.341 --> 00:52:35.235
yet still, you might think

00:52:35.236 --> 00:52:37.495
they shouldn't compound
that injustice,

00:52:37.496 --> 00:52:38.960
that an injustice had been done

00:52:38.961 --> 00:52:40.510
to someone and there's something

00:52:40.511 --> 00:52:42.210
morally troubling about carrying

00:52:42.211 --> 00:52:43.990
that injustice into
another domain.

00:52:43.991 --> 00:52:46.260
Now, not only the woman
got beaten up,

00:52:46.261 --> 00:52:48.620
but now she can't get
health or life insurance.

00:52:48.621 --> 00:52:51.980
So, I think there are a lot
of situations that raise

00:52:51.981 --> 00:52:53.750
those kinds of questions
that I think are

00:52:53.751 --> 00:52:56.080
important to think about.

00:52:56.081 --> 00:52:57.870
&gt;&gt; I wanted to ask a question

00:52:57.871 --> 00:53:00.695
back to the original idea
about fairness.

00:53:00.696 --> 00:53:04.530
So, I have a lot of concern
about facial recognition ever

00:53:04.531 --> 00:53:08.040
being used on balance
better or well,

00:53:08.041 --> 00:53:12.445
for society than on balance
injuring as a society.

00:53:12.446 --> 00:53:14.070
So, one of the things
that I'm thinking

00:53:14.071 --> 00:53:15.640
a lot about is when does

00:53:15.641 --> 00:53:17.190
a company or when does

00:53:17.191 --> 00:53:20.510
a technologist that
develops a system say,

00:53:20.511 --> 00:53:23.010
"This isn't going
to work in the way,

00:53:23.011 --> 00:53:25.550
there's too much
unfairness and so,

00:53:25.551 --> 00:53:28.270
I'm just cutting
bait, it's done."

00:53:28.271 --> 00:53:29.780
I would like to know
what the metric,

00:53:29.781 --> 00:53:31.330
if that's ever happened in

00:53:31.331 --> 00:53:33.250
your experience and
what the metric is?

00:53:33.251 --> 00:53:35.110
I'll just give you
an example in law,

00:53:35.111 --> 00:53:37.730
there was a brief time
when the death penalty

00:53:37.731 --> 00:53:42.260
was determined to be
not working the way we,

00:53:42.261 --> 00:53:45.100
as a society, needed it to work.

00:53:45.101 --> 00:53:49.425
So, we stopped in the United
States administering it.

00:53:49.426 --> 00:53:51.090
We've now gone back
to it thinking

00:53:51.091 --> 00:53:52.915
that it's now working better,

00:53:52.916 --> 00:53:56.095
but there were metrics by
which that was being measured.

00:53:56.096 --> 00:53:57.930
We could agree or disagree
about whether those are

00:53:57.931 --> 00:53:59.910
the right metrics and
whether it's still working,

00:53:59.911 --> 00:54:03.084
but I'd like to hear from
a technology perspective,

00:54:03.085 --> 00:54:06.905
whether there is
an indication that it is

00:54:06.906 --> 00:54:09.280
so unfair or the outcome is

00:54:09.281 --> 00:54:12.355
so wrong that we can't fix it.

00:54:12.356 --> 00:54:14.830
&gt;&gt; I, well.

00:54:14.960 --> 00:54:17.880
&gt;&gt; It's a very good question.

00:54:17.881 --> 00:54:20.570
I'm thinking a lot
about this right

00:54:20.571 --> 00:54:22.835
now because, for example,

00:54:22.836 --> 00:54:26.470
when our paper with
Joyce paper first came out,

00:54:26.471 --> 00:54:29.580
what some people were saying was,

00:54:29.581 --> 00:54:31.685
"So, why are you
trying to fix it?"

00:54:31.686 --> 00:54:34.750
Because we know that
for African-Americans,

00:54:34.751 --> 00:54:37.500
for example, it's
being used unfairly.

00:54:37.501 --> 00:54:39.690
So, even if it works perfectly,

00:54:39.691 --> 00:54:42.970
should we have this technology?

00:54:42.971 --> 00:54:45.860
I have a lot of
thoughts about this,

00:54:45.861 --> 00:54:49.130
but I think the two things
are separate, right?

00:54:49.131 --> 00:54:52.440
Like, there's one thing where
you're showing disparity

00:54:52.441 --> 00:54:55.860
in how something works and
then the second thing is,

00:54:55.861 --> 00:54:58.035
what that thing is
being used for.

00:54:58.036 --> 00:55:00.400
So, in one way,

00:55:00.401 --> 00:55:02.315
the fact that we
showed disparity,

00:55:02.316 --> 00:55:04.230
I think, helps people now

00:55:04.231 --> 00:55:06.670
say we need to have legislation.

00:55:06.671 --> 00:55:08.275
For example, in
California, right?

00:55:08.276 --> 00:55:10.720
They said there was
legislation passed on

00:55:10.721 --> 00:55:14.035
the senate floor to say that,

00:55:14.036 --> 00:55:17.365
before law enforcement uses
face recognition technology,

00:55:17.366 --> 00:55:21.200
we have to audit
it in California.

00:55:21.201 --> 00:55:23.750
So, right now that's
not happening.

00:55:23.751 --> 00:55:25.040
Everybody was just using face

00:55:25.041 --> 00:55:27.065
recognition technology
the way they wanted it.

00:55:27.066 --> 00:55:29.240
But at least showing
this disparity now starts to

00:55:29.241 --> 00:55:31.555
have the conversation
where the public

00:55:31.556 --> 00:55:33.530
and our elected officials
and everybody

00:55:33.531 --> 00:55:35.360
else can actually at least make

00:55:35.361 --> 00:55:37.520
a decision about whether we want

00:55:37.521 --> 00:55:39.995
to use it in certain
use cases or not.

00:55:39.996 --> 00:55:42.650
But, I don't support

00:55:42.651 --> 00:55:44.790
a complete ban on face
recognition technology

00:55:44.791 --> 00:55:47.480
under any circumstances
whatsoever.

00:55:47.481 --> 00:55:49.170
I mean, I might change my mind

00:55:49.171 --> 00:55:52.455
if I talked to other people
about this because,

00:55:52.456 --> 00:55:55.445
there are some cases
in which, for example,

00:55:55.446 --> 00:55:56.880
when Affectiva by that emotion to

00:55:56.881 --> 00:55:59.320
recognition technology
was started,

00:55:59.321 --> 00:56:01.825
Hanna talks about how she
was working with kids,

00:56:01.826 --> 00:56:04.160
with autistic kids
and how this was

00:56:04.161 --> 00:56:07.115
helping kids with autism.

00:56:07.116 --> 00:56:09.470
People have written
about older people with

00:56:09.471 --> 00:56:11.720
dementia and how some of
this technology might help.

00:56:11.721 --> 00:56:14.870
So, I think we

00:56:14.871 --> 00:56:16.835
have to look at it on
a case-by-case basis.

00:56:16.836 --> 00:56:19.380
For certain applications,
like for example,

00:56:19.381 --> 00:56:21.005
screening people at the airport.

00:56:21.006 --> 00:56:22.610
There's too many false-positives,

00:56:22.611 --> 00:56:25.180
I don't think it should be
used for that purpose, right?

00:56:25.181 --> 00:56:27.540
Then, there maybe there's
other applications

00:56:27.541 --> 00:56:30.405
for which you might still
want to use it for.

00:56:30.406 --> 00:56:31.850
&gt;&gt; Eric.

00:56:31.851 --> 00:56:33.810
&gt;&gt; It's a good question
you asked about, you know,

00:56:33.811 --> 00:56:36.240
how do we make
these decisions and just in

00:56:36.241 --> 00:56:38.680
terms of a couple of cases people

00:56:38.681 --> 00:56:39.690
here might know about

00:56:39.691 --> 00:56:41.240
the Aether Committee
at Microsoft and

00:56:41.241 --> 00:56:45.010
advisory board that we
have Mira and Hanna,

00:56:45.011 --> 00:56:48.740
serving on, the folks in

00:56:48.741 --> 00:56:52.380
that board helped craft
the block that was

00:56:52.381 --> 00:56:54.050
written on face recognition

00:56:54.051 --> 00:56:58.250
and there was some recent cases

00:56:58.251 --> 00:56:59.505
that have come to the board,

00:56:59.506 --> 00:57:02.400
where Microsoft, the board
itself and then the

00:57:02.401 --> 00:57:08.225
senior to Microsoft
said, "That's okay.

00:57:08.226 --> 00:57:10.145
You know something? The scenario,

00:57:10.146 --> 00:57:11.600
we're just not comfortable with,

00:57:11.601 --> 00:57:14.179
we're not doing that contract."

00:57:14.260 --> 00:57:18.050
There's a reason, a reasoning
that goes on, typically,

00:57:18.051 --> 00:57:21.205
it's not metric-based or
accuracy-based in this case,

00:57:21.206 --> 00:57:24.870
it's we don't understand
the usage well enough and

00:57:24.871 --> 00:57:26.490
how it's going to
work in terms of

00:57:26.491 --> 00:57:29.585
its implications
for human rights,

00:57:29.586 --> 00:57:32.290
freedom of expression,
freedom of assembly,

00:57:32.291 --> 00:57:34.495
how the errors are going
to work out in your life,

00:57:34.496 --> 00:57:36.710
we have to study that further.

00:57:36.711 --> 00:57:38.970
Sometimes, it doesn't
get down to accuracy,

00:57:38.971 --> 00:57:40.405
it's like even even perfectly

00:57:40.406 --> 00:57:41.825
accurate facial recognition,

00:57:41.826 --> 00:57:43.695
perfectly fair bio-metric.

00:57:43.696 --> 00:57:46.320
Let's say, one over
several people

00:57:46.321 --> 00:57:49.610
on the panel might agree with,

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:53.360
even perfect recognition is that,

00:57:53.361 --> 00:57:55.570
it can cause a chilling effect
when it comes to

00:57:55.571 --> 00:57:58.160
freedom of expression
and freedom of assembly,

00:57:58.161 --> 00:57:59.890
